      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>CDOUG I .5tS PATENT SELF 243. A ISIN(Jr F ONCE TRIED, ALWAYS USED.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTE.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>There are so many things in the way of dress to the front now that it is really difficult for me to know what exactly to begin with, for there is every fear that in the coming season there will be pre-ponderous of the over dressed. There is a tendency in the current fashions for the fair sex to look as if they had been drawn through a ring, but it is by no means in the beat taste to over do this, to havo skirts too long on the ground, sleeves falling too much over the fingers, toques too broad across the face, with an over luxuriance of feathers. In all departments of dress the amount of work required is simply astonishing, and brims and revers are alike covered with many tuckings. (04</p>
        <p>The pinnaeled hat shows a return to the high pointed crowns, and is made in drawn silk, the large bows of silk giving width, emphasised by the wings that stand out on either side. • Here there is any amount of work, and the colourings are title, le and cerise, with a touch of black in the trimmings, and white ospreys It is made in a certain satin rush, difficult to obtain. The brims are all important, as may be gleaned from the toque, Nlehich has full rufll3d bouillonnet of silk below, caught up by the jewelled buckle, the up- standing ostrich feather rising from amid soft' chiffon. It is a very' becoming style, after the order of Rembrandt. The Pompadour element asserts itseif greatly in millinery, and both chiffon and silk having a white ground display stnall printed natural-tinted flowers all over. This is employed not only round the crowns, but under- neath. There are some delightful exemplifications of the Toreador style, which is likely to prevail, and there is no doubt that any amount of roses of every colour conceivable will be the fashion. I have seen many examples lately of millinery, which seems to take an altogether new direction since we turned our attention to spring modes. A really pretty notion is an upturned brim covered with rose leaves, which are made to lie perfectly flat; if they stood out they would be fussy and unbecoming, but deftly placed, they are simply charming.</p>
        <p>Apropos of fashion, I may say that among the many new spring models the Princesse robe and tunique sty lea are varied by the appearance of the becoming and always popular bolero, the back cut en Princesse or in redingote fashion. One is a ilette de promenade made in light mordoriS Venetian cloth; the bolero fronts are caught together by a strap of cloth in front, fastened with small crystal buttons, whilst cut out paille coloured cloth appears as an ornamentation on either side, finishing in a gold narrow buckle towards the one side. The close fitting skirt forms a fl ,t pleat a la redingote at the back, and is cut up at the side, displaying a panel embroi- dered with shaded mordore panne and gold, around which is the light cloth appliquti which also ornaments the base. The semi-blouse of white and pink finely striped silk is pleated into a ruche in trout, edged with boSbe black ribbon velvet. The hat, having its brim of gathered tulle edged with straw, is ornamented with pink moss roses and white plumes, completing one of the most dibtingude Elbow sleeves have again returned to favour with a less Parisian air than the extravagantly long transparent sleeves which have been fashion- able in demi-toilettes. The new elbow sleeve is excessively tight and close and the rucked or ruffled sleeve re-appears in elbow length, and tucks, cordings, and lines of gaugings are all re- peated in the shorter length. Some of them are made of silk or firm material, such as cashmere, nun's veiling, &amp;c., and are tucked in groups, and finished with a graduated frill of lace. There is a small square epaulette, outlined with silk ruching, which makes a smart finish, and this sleeve can be made with epaulettes of rounded or oval shape, and without the tucks, if desired.</p>
        <p>Very pretty blouse bodices are being made for evening wear. Let me describe one of the very latest. It was of rose pink mousseline de soie, the bodice puffed across and separated by serpentine lace insertion highly spangled with silver. The bodice was cut in a very large square, but two rows of black velvet ribbon are :brought over the bare shoulders and fasten on to the bodice with small paste buckles back and front. • The waist- band is of velvet, ra9tened by a bow and buckle at .the side. The sleeves are of lace and mousseline de sole, forming one puff on the shoulder, and a series of snis.ll puff down the back to the wrist, where a frill falls over the hand. There are some smart tailor-made coats and skirts, being a happy combination of black and white, the well•cut skirt of shepherd's plaid, and the trim little coat of black suiting faced with plaid and lined with silk. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>TO DRIVE AWAY MlOE.—Powdered camphor placed in the haunts of mice will drive them away, as they dislike the smell.</p>
