      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meting of this Council was held ha the Board I Felled's, Great Senkey,on Monday evening. There were I present Messrs. J. Wright, T. Halton, William Taylor, I Thomas Kilshaw. G. Thompson, Thomas Cooke, with Mr. J. Smith (clerk). THE REPAIRS OF FOOTPATHS.—IMPORTANT • CORRESPONDENCE. The Clerk said he addres,ed the following letter to the local landowners on the question of the footpaths : "At the last meeting of the Council, the queition of keeping field footpaths in repair was brought for- ward. The Council after fully considering such question resolved that the several landowners coecerned should be written to on the subject and requested to undertake such repairs. The fielu footpaths in thi-; township are rather numerous ; but if treated individually the cost of keeping them in repair would be very light. The Council therefore venture to hope that you will see your way in the interests of all concerned (and of cellists without prejudice) to make arrangements for the foot- paths through your land to be kept in repair. Similar communications are being addressed to the other land- owners of this township, and a favourable reply is re- spectfully solicited," He had received four letters in reply to the communication. Mr. Henry Greene!l on behalf of the Blue Coat Trustees acknowledged the re- ceipt of it, and Mr. Selby, agent for Lord Lilferd, said he would Rive the matter his attention. Mr. W. D.Jeans wrote "I have permitted the public to tres- pass by walking down my back road and thence over a small bit of lard to the field belonging to Mr. Hughes in the occupation of Mr. Pownall but I do not know that I am under any obligation to make a good road for the public accommodation or convenience through my property. I would rather they would think the road too bad to go that way and en some other. I should be quite willing, however, if the Parish Council can induce Mr. Hughes to do so to concur in opering cut a new and more convenient cart and foot roadway from the in- tended road or tereet running between the Board Schools and the Sueday schools thrcugh my land and thence through Mr. Hughes' land to Heath-read. This would effect a great imprevement and public convenience and do away with the present bad road over Mr. Hughes' land. Ihat% a right of cart road and otherwise over Mr. Hughes land under the Greystone Heath enclosure award as appurtenant to my property but the public have no sucti right."—Mr- W. Knowles, of Widnes. wrote " A:though the object of ssction 13 01 the Local Government Act of 1894 is no doubt to enable Parish councils to maintain footpaths which are seecially service ab'e to parishioners se long as the Councils do no excced their limit cf ex- penditure, still I do not see that they have any power to require other persons to maintain or repair. I have not sea:fled myself yet whetter tt.ere is a right of way across the farm beloneng to Messrs. Knowles along which the pubic are entitled to go nor under what cen- dition the persons or public (if any) were allowed to cross ncr whether there was claimed to be a right of way trfore the Highway Act of 1835. Under the cir- cumstances I don't feel just fit d in advising Messrs. Knowles to tecr•gnin by repairing any field tracks, if any, as many of the field tracks in the cow try have ro legal existence and it would-be inadvisable to formally recegnize their exis. rence. I also do not feel justified in putting Messrs- Knowles to any expense by makieg inquiries. Perhaps you could kindly give me replies to the point and par- ticulars mentioned in this letter. I cannot of course re. cogniz • your right to ma ke ot repair a Uld track but i you wish to enter into negetiaticm fer so doing I will be pleased to consider the matte:. Kindly say what is the ground of claim and complaint in this matter and on what (if any) legal authority The Clerk then read tome points cf law referring to the repair of footle, ths Mr. Halten asked if it was a fact that any farmer in the neighbeurbood of GI eat Sankey ploughed up the footpath -across his field. The Chairman replied that that had been dense Pie Clerk informed the Council that he lad written to 13 laneen-ners altogether, and he had only received replies from the gentlemen named. Strange to say, all the lettsr4 ve re from solicltore, land? Mr. Hasten . Has a termer a right to plough up the The Chairman ; Idc n't Mirk so. The Clerk : W here a arise can prove that frcm time immfreerial he has plcu4bed the footpath, even though he may I ave allowed a f. w years W elapse, be has a right, in the elnence .f dcfinire prcof, to do so. Mr. Baton: And anyone se,eed justifit din cross- ing tee fled in the circumstances ?</p>
