      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>Mr. Thompson thought this was a matter for the Rural District Council to take into consideration. He considered that they were very much indebted to Mr. Smith for the trouble he had taken in the matter, and reading them so many legal opinions about it. They had made the matter very simple. He could not see that the Council could take any responsibility in the matter.</p>
        <p>Ths Clerk: You are opening up a wide question. The Chairman said it would be a great injustice to make the farmers repair the footpaths running through their fields.</p>
        <p>Mr. Halton: The Parish Council cannot make the farmers who are the tenants repair the footpaths.</p>
        <p>The Chmirman They have tried it on at Penketh.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thompson said fo-otpaths were a great nuisance and loss to farmers already and they ought act t) Le called upon by landlords to keep them in repair. Mr. Cooke Farmers would be very, glad to be with- out footpaths. The Clerk said a member of the Council had received a com-.nunication which stated that Mr Thompson, the Highway Board's Surveyor, bad repaired footpaths from Geoid to Houghton Green'and to Blackbrook for the last few years. Ha had got anyone whom he could to cart the cinders and sent h-s men to spread them. After turther discussion, Mr. Halton said he would be pleased to iaise the question of footpaths before the Rural District Council. It was decided that the Clerk be instructed to write to the Rural District Councii of Warrington inforn.ing them that Mr. Halton would in- trodtue the matter to their notice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>TRIENNIAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>TRIENNIAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that he had written to the Local Government Board, as rzquested, and to the County Council. pointing out Me desirability of eke:ions of Parish Councillors being held triennially, itutead of annually, and also concurrently with elections of Guar- dians and Rural District Councillors. The Local Govern- ment Board had acknowledged the letter, and the C ,un, y Council had promised to lay the matter before the Parliamentary Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>TRE BOUNDARY TO BE WALKED.</head>
        <p>TRE BOUNDARY TO BE WALKED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Halton spokb ot the desirability of walking tha boundary at no distant period. If it were done it would be useful to the members of the Council, as many did not know where the boundary was. At the same time, inquiries could be made as to the ownership of the foot- paths ; and whether any had been intrfered with and pho:rhed up. Be was sure a benefit would be derived by walking the boundary. Mr. Thompson asked who would know where the bout dary was. flit iterk reclird that the Ordnance map would show it, but if they dccided to walk the boundary it would be n (eq3ary in their rambles to walk across the canal. iLlughier.) Yr. Haitm : We should have to get a boat._</p>
        <p>• On the rnotion of Mr. Thompson, seconded by Mr. /1114 haw it 'was decided to laye the matter in the hands of the Chairman and Mr. Salton, to fix a date for the walking of the boundary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>INTERNATIONAL CRUSADE OF PEACE.</head>
        <p>THE INTERNATIONAL CRUSADE OF PEACE.</p>
        <p>A circular letter on the above Euhject was prented by the Clerk. It was &amp;Tided to tali(' no action with regard to it, txcept to acknowledge its receipt. Mr. Thompson explaining that a meeting in ftvour of the peac proposals had already been held in Warrington.</p>
