      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, MARCH 24, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, MARCH 24, 1899.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The Queen's life at Cimiez is much the same as when the Court is at Osborne, or Balmoral. After b eakfast —which is usually taken in the garden—comes a tour of the grounds, undertaken in Her Majery's phaeton, drawn by the little white donkey "Jacko." Next comes luncbeon, audit the weather be propitious, a lengthy drive in the afternoon. Tea is frequently partaken of in the open air, the carriage being drawn up by the way- side, in some delightful spit. Then cAlles dinner, after which Her Majesty spends an hour with her guests, re- tiring to her own epic tments about eleven o'clock. The Princess of Wales seems to be thoroughly enjoying her cruise, and is already looking very much brighter for the change. In her simple yachting dress, she loAs younger and prettier than ev( r, and certainly no one would t*lte her to be the mothsr of the two matronly- looking princesses who are aceompansing her on her travels. Her Royal Highness is an excellent linguist, but of all languages she speaks German best perhaps. _ .</p>
        <p>:t will be riPmemriered that her m ther, the late Queen</p>
        <p>of Denmark, was a German, and as the Princess has naturally had much intercourse with German princes and princesses at our own Court, it is scarcely to be marvelled at that she speaks the language of the Father- land with greater ease than any other The Princess of Wales' favourite dressing case, which always accompanies her wherever she gees, is a most exquisitely fitted affair. The case itself is of rosewood, and contains a chased silver-gilt toilet service and mirror. The decoration of this elaborate set is of the Queen Ann period, the mirror, which fits into the upper portion of the rosewood case, being very beautifully framed. Along the edge of the upper part of the case, when open, is seen an engraved, continuous riband. or scroll rn which is an inscription. The case, which is lined with Royal blue velvet, has a gi t handle at each side. Princess Frederica of Hanover, who has been eaying at Biarritz intends being at Gmiinden for her mother's eighty-first birthday. The Queen of Hanover was married to the blind King, when twenty-five years of age. She was a splendid wife to him in the early and unclouded years of their married life, and latterly when war and disaster fell on their Kingd im, she was a true helpmeet and counsellor. After the battles of IBR6, when their kingdom was incorporated with Prussia,aed and when all was lost, she accempanied her husband ir.- to an exile which has lasted ever since. The residence of the Queen is the Villa Thein at Gmiinden. in Upper Austria,within a few minutes drive of Schloss Gmiinden the residence of her son, the Duke of Cumberland. She is very fond of youeg peogle, and still likes to have music, and a little dancing in the evening. Princes Frederica is a great favourite with our Queen, who delights in her society. Nearly twenty years ago the princess married Baron Alphrnse Von Pawel Ram- mingen, but the alliance was much against the wishes of her relatiens.in spite of the partisanship of the Queen who offended the Duke of Cumberland by being present at his sister's marriage iii St. George's Chapel, Windsor. The Prince sa had elle daughter, who died in iefancy,and %These tiny coffin lies on that of her grandfather, the King of Hanover, under St. George's Chapel. The baby was called Victoria... Miss Maud Valerie White, who has probably made more men( y by her songs than any other British Wunian composer, was, like Sir Arthur Sullivan, a winner of the English Mendelssohn scholarship, founded in memory of the great crmposer. Miss White studied her art in England and Germany, and her earliest successes were achieved by her settings of the verses of the standard poets. She is a very et elcome and familiar figure in many a London salon.</p>
        <p>Among the American women composors, the most ambitious is certainly Mrs. H. H. A. Beach. Her " Juti. late for the decication of the Women's Bulding at the Co'umtinn Exposition" is indeed a sort of clarion nc,te of triumph. Mrs. Beach (Amy Marcy Cheney) was born in New Hampshire. Her descent is American back to colonial times. She composed as early as the age of four, and with the exception of a preliminary COUlee in har- mony, has herself to credit for her thorough knowledge of musical thcory. She translated the works of Berlicz and Savaert for her own instruction in instrumentation. Mrs. Beach is a pianist also, having given public per- formances since the age of sixteen... .</p>
        <p>No one N‘ ho aspires to be up-to-date, cycles now-a- da3 tr, instead the fashionat le woman walk.. The fad, if it lasts, is likely to prove extremely beneficial to both health and figure. The Duchess of Devonshire, and the Duchess of Portland, vialk for a couple of brurs daily. and Lady Randolph Churchill, Lady Wolverton. Lady Ge,ard, Lady Powys, Lady Constance Coombe, Madame Van Andre, and Mrs. Langtry, all follow th( ir excellent example, more or less. There is no finer exereiee than walking, and we should bear less about d 3 spei sia, and nerves, if it were more widely indulged in. Braiding will be very fashionable this spring and coming summer, and the woman who is desirous of renovating a gown of the tailor made persuasion, has easy t mes before her. By decorating the revers, adding scrolls to the skit And front and back seem of the coat, it it an easy matter to render a frock .f Lirly recant build alrnobt unrecognisable. And in these days—when to be seen more than a few times in the same gown is a cil crime—this is no inconsiderable matter.</p>
        <p>Slk shirts promise to be more worn thlip ever this year. Thow already exhibited are particularly dainty. and more carefully thought out than hitherto. There is no doubt that this particular garment is a boon and a blessing to womenkind. With a well built coat and skirt one can ring the chaeges in a most delightful manner, if equipped with two or three vests, to wear with the ccat, and a couple of dainty shirts. It is generally supposed that Royalty and well-knawn society people are responsible for new modes, tuf such is not the case. By tar the larger number are the result of aceidents or ideas suggested by something altogether ulterior. The weather plays an important parC in set- ting the fashion. For example, a spell of excessive cold la this country a few years ago was responsible for the adoption by our sex of the flat soled, low-heeled boot. Skating WAS possible for a much longer las riod than is usual, and this boot, which is the best for the eport,wam found to be so comfortable that society decided it uas the correct thing for walking. Spats were generally adopted at the same time by scciety. prey OU3 to which period they were of thin material, and were worn for appearance rather than comfort. Strckings this spring ate, generally speaking, in the best of taste. Very showy ones are being offered at a low price, but as over-bright colors, arid scrawly pat- terns in embroideries are not only ugly, but very enlarg- ing to the fret, we should do well to avoid them. Women with large feet should always wear bleck or dark coloured stockings, and should never have any embroi- dery upon them unless it is a simple dot or speck of color. Thin wco'en stockings are the hea tbiest for win- ter wear, but a theavy make of spun silk is the most pleasant. MACARONI CHEESE iS an appetising supper dish, if nic( ly prepared. The necessary ingredients are, quarter of a pound of macaroni, three ounces of grated cheese, one ounce of butter, two tablespoonfuls of milk, a little Bali and a pinch of cayenne. Break the macaroni into pieces, and lay it in a pan, cover with water, boil for ten or fifteen minutes. When strained add a pint of milk, and simmer one hour, till tender. When cooked place in another pan, with pepper, salt, c4yenne, or milk, and half the grated cheese. Let it become hot, but it must not boil. Turn into a flat dish, spread the remainder of the cheese on top, and brown in the oven cr before a brisk fire. Parmesan answers better than any other cheese for this dish.</p>
        <p>" MADG E."</p>
