      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>(Copyright.)</head>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>PRINCESS DELILAH.</p>
        <p>By MRS. GEORGE CORBETT, Author of "When the Sea Give; up its Dead," "The Prince Physician," "The Vindication of Oh ive Trevethick," "The Star of Yukon," "The Adventures of an Ugly Girl," "Deb o' Melly'," "A Fair Blockade Runner," ite.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL SIREN.</p>
        <p>"How did you come here, dear one?" inquired Mr. Price, after the first rapture of the meeting was over. "This house is full of peril, and it is not a safe place for you to be in.,, :'Nor for you, either, lam sure. Let us leave rt at once, before it is too late. Why, you are wounded! You are bleeding! And you aro in need of help! Come home with me. My house- keeper is a capital nurse, and she will know exactly what to do for you, unless, indeed, your round is serious."</p>
        <p>"Oh, no, it is only very slight. But I think I have lost a great deal of blood, and it is making me feel rather faint."</p>
        <p>"All the more reason why you should be attended to without delay. Besides, you have a lot of bloodstains about you, and they might lead to your arrest. I can guess how you came by them, for I have seen the two men who are gagged and bound in the other room, and I knew before I saw you that you must have had a dreadful struggle with them." "Do, you know who they are ? " _</p>
        <p>"I dO now. I discovei:ed all about them after you left the Countess's rooms; and when I learnt what sort of place it was to which I had sent you, I nearly went mad until I saw you again. But come; if we are caught here, think how serious the situation will be for both of us."</p>
        <p>This reminder helped to pull Mr. Price's wits together a little. "Good Heavens!" he exclaimed. "What am I thinking of to linger here, and run the risk of ruining your reputation for ever? In another instant we will set out for the nearest police office to give information." "You cannot do that. You must see me home first; then you can explain everything without bringing me into the matter at all. But how can we go out like this? Your clothes are marked with blood in several places, and—look at this!" _</p>
        <p>Mr. Price did look-, and saw some dark stains on the cloak with which the Princess had covered the beautiful evening gown she was wearing. "Suppose we are surprised here," the lady observed, with either real or well simulat.d nervousness, "these stains will be sure to be dis- covered, and I shall be just as sure to be placed in an unpleasant predicament through' them., ' , "We must prevent that at all costs. Luckily for me, I had taken off my light overcoat and left it downstairs when I was asked to come up into the presence-chamber. I will button it close up to my neck when I go out. It will, perhaps, hide all tell-tale evidences of the fray. Then, if you gather your cloak closely round you, no one will see that it is blood-stained, and I will escort you safely home before I take any further steps." A moment later, the couple walked through the so-called presence-chamber on their way out of the house, and saw the two Arabs lying where Mr. Price had left them, their furious eyes blazing hate and revenge at him as the Princess urged him past with almost frenzie.Qpeed. "They are bound securely enough," he thought, "and can stay where they are until I have seen the Princess safely home. Then I will go and give information to the police." Perhaps some fresh doubts might have assailed Mr. Price if he had known that both the nip had it in their power to move if they chose, an'd that he was barely out of the house before each of them was on his feet, doing his best to get rid of the effects of the punishment he had received. It would also have still further astonished the re- infatuated lover if he could have known that the bonds by which he had secured the captives had been deliberately cut by no less a person than the Princess Delilah.</p>
        <p>But, all unconscious of the extent to which he was once more being duped, he opened the door, looked cautiously up and down the street to make sure that there was no one about, and emerged from the house with his companion, softly closing the door behind him. Then, carefully wrapped up, they walked quickly towards the nearest cab- stand, where they found a hansom disengaged, and were speedily driven to the Princess's residence. Mr. Price had scruples about actually entering the house with her at so late an hour. But the lady herself appeared to think much less of the rules of conventionality than she did of the necessity of assurini, herself that her lover was not seriously wounded, and she insisted upon his at once yielding himself to the ministrations of her companion-housekeeper, a woman of little conversation but much skill, who set about her work 'without evincing the slightest surprise at its nature.</p>
        <p>The wounds which had been inflicted by the knife of the assassin shewed themselves very amenable to treatment, for, although they had certainly bled freely for some time, there was nothing about them which plenty of sticking- plaister would not mend. The woman, whom the Princess called Sarena, had now finished her task, and Mr. Price, feel- •' ing himself again, was apologising profusely for all the trouble he was causing. But his apologies were received with deprecating smiles by his beautiful hostess, who insisted upon his taking some refresh- ment before he went out again. 'l‘Those men are safe enough," she said, "and cannot possibly do any more mischief at present. Nor 'will it hurt them to stay where they are for an hour longer; and, if it comes to that, they deserve all the punishment they have got, for treating you so shamefully. But you haven't told me yet how it all happened." Thus invited, Mr. Price unfolded the whole tale of his adventures to the Princess, while she sat by his side, holding his hand in hers, and ever and anon protesting her thankfulness for his miraculous escape. When he had finished, she promised to explain what it was that she ;had learnt concerning Madame Celia.</p>
        <p>"But not until we have had a little supper," she concluded. "I don't know how you feel, but I feel desperately in want of something to eat, for I hardly ate anything at dinner to-night, and all the excitement has told upon me." "Well, now you mention it," was the reply, "I daresay I shall be none the worse for a taste of the supper you have so kindly provided for me, and, as you say, consideration for those two scoundrels whom I tied up is not a virtue which we need to cultivate."</p>
        <p>"Of course it isn't. But try a little of this ileidsieck ; there is nothing in the world like it for bracing one's nerves." Mr. Price was not a man who was given to excess, either in eating or in drinking, and some of his friends, more convivially inclined than him- self, had often laughed at him because he never allowed himself to exceed a second glass of champagne„ On the present occasion he main- tained his usual limit,, yet somehow, perhaps in consequence of the nervous excitement he had experienced, he became suddenly drowsy, and though he fought against tilt sensation with all the strength of mind and body which he could muster, it overcame him, and he actually fell asleep in the presence of a lady whom he considered beauti- ful enough and fascinating enough to enchain the attention of all beholders. "There! " exclaimed the Princess, "that task is over! What a fool the man is, to be sure! A veritable baby could manage him. Sarena 1 " "Yes, madame," was the instant reply of the housekeeper, who entered the room at this moment in response to a sammons from a little gong which stood on a table in the room.</p>
        <p>"Put a couple of cushions under that man's head, and raise his feet on to the lounge, so that he will not roll off. Then come and attend to me in my own room. Our friends will not be here until nine or ten in the morning, so you must take care that the patient is not seen by anyone but yourself; and you need not be afraid that he will awake before we are ready, for him." Having given these orders, the Princess Delilah retired to rest, with as little compunction concerning the man whom she had tricked and deceived as she would have felt for the sorrows of a sick mouse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHAPTER XXII. IN THE TRAP.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXII. IN THE TRAP.</p>
        <p>The trio of adventurers in Hamlet House were sanguine of immediate success, and Mr. Rizzen- Quiche was so excited that his companions were afraid he would betray - himself before their guide .was effectually disposed of. "I say," suddenly exclaimed Lord Alfred, "don't ladies always make a great deal of fuss about cupboards, and that sort o/ thing? I know</p>
        <p>that my people have hacl quite ts number of them built into their town house. We had better have a look round here again before we go and give our report upon this place." "So we had," agreed Mr. Raleigh. "Perhaps this man will be able to shew us anything we may have missed."</p>
        <p>The man alluded to seemed by no means averse to being as obliging as possible now, and if he had known that the object was to induce him to turn his back to the supposed house-hunters he could not have played into their hands more cleverly, for he walked past Lord Alfred with a great show of zeal. But he gave the latter no time to carry out his intention of grasping him by the neck, so as to stop any outcry he might be disposed to make while he was being rendered unequal to the task of notifying to outsiders the fact that something unusual was transpiring in Hamlet House. A sudden slip caused the fellow to fall headlong at the foot of the stairs, and a sharp exclamation of pain was intended to convey the information that he had met with a severe injury of some sort.</p>
        <p>"Oh, dear! " he moaned. "I believe I have broken my ankle! Curse it, that's what comes of running after other folks! I wish I had never seen them. 0-oh! 0-oh! Whatever shall Ido with it ? " "I am afraid you will have to put up with the pain for a little while, my man," was Lord Alfred's cool reply, "for we are duffers at surgery. Can you go upstairs with us, do you think ? " "Oh, laws, no! I can't stir a peg! 0-oh! 0-oh!" "Well, in that case, it won't hurt you to be prevented from injuring your foot by moving, so here you are." And in another second the dilapidated-looking mortal was lying on his back at the foot of the stairs, so effectually gagged and bound that only by the aid of others could he ever hope to liberate himself again. He made as good a show of resist- tnce as his disadvantageous position would permit, managed to splutter out an oath or two before els mouth was muffled. But it was a case of three o one, and his captors were soon bounding upstairs, feeling secure against betrayal or interruption, and confident of finding the locked rooms occupied by the persons they sought. This time there was no whispering, but a bold knock, accompanied by a clearly spoken inquiry. "Are you there, Teddy ? " asked Mr. Rizzen- Quicke, his heart beating anxiously as he listened for the reply, which came in the form of a pro- longed groan. "Open the door," urged Lord Alfred, but the answer was an even more melancholy groan than the first.</p>
        <p>"We must break the door in," suggest-a Mr. Raleigh. "One cannot stand on ceremony i cases like this, and it is easy to guess that either weak- ness or a gag prevents the men inside from giving us a proper answer." -Mr. Rizzen-Quicke was already pushing hard at the door, and it was already cracking at the binges. A simultaneous attack by all three men completed the operation, and as the door gave way beneath the weight brought to bear upon it they all found themselves precipitated forward, with more speed than dignity. "It's all right, sir," exclaimed Lord Alfred cheerfully. "This is only an ante-room, and we shall have to penetrate a little further, that's all." Straight opposite the door which had just been forced was another similar one, which was attacked it once, and with even greater hopefulness, for the groans sounded much nearer now, and seemed more like the ineffectual utterances of a muffled voice than that of a person who suffered from excessive bodily weakness, thus encouraging the theory that gags had been employed. The new barrier afforded even less resistance than the first one had done, and almost before they had time to realise what was happening the invaders found themselves in the inner of the two rooms, struggling in the grip of three powerful men, who were fully prepared to receive them. So utterly unexpected was the attack that its result was a foregone conclusion, and all three were helpless prisoners almost in the twink- ling of an eye. "Fools!" exclaimed a scornful voice in their ears. "Did you think you could match your wits against ours with impunity? And did you think that the role of amateur detective was a safe one to take up? If so, perhaps you already realise your error, and I assure, you, gentlemen, that by the time we have done with you, you will acknowledge yourselves beaten. You are in search of certain persons in whom you are interested, and you allowed the Princess Delilah to befool you into thinking that they would be found here! Truly, it does not take much to gull the person of average intellect! " "The Duke of Hapsburg-Solltesein! " was the almost simultaneous exclamation of the three betricked men, who found no difficulty in compre- hending the situation as soon as the Princess Delilah's name was mentioned. She and Madame Celia were evidently one and the same person, and as a pretended clairvoyante she utilised the social gossip and information which her position as a Princess enabled her to gather. When the blotting paper, the seal, and the walking-stick were shewn to her, she knew that she had dangerous enemies, and she hit upon a clever plan of decoying them to a lonely house which had somehow come under her notice. After this, the rest would be easy. • Certain that her dupes would not fail to visit Hamlet House, she prepared a warm reception for them, and the presence of the Duke solved all doubts as to whether he and she were accomplices or not.</p>
        <p>Unfortunately, it also pointed- to certainty in another direction. If he and his companions enter- tained any prospect of the captives making their escape again, they would not risk shewing them- selves without disguise, and it was therefore self- evident that their designs for the immediate future were nothing short of murderous. The cunning manner in which they had brought about the capture had precluded any chance of one of the victims either escaping through the outer door or giving such a noisy alarm as to attract attention, for this part of the house was the most secluded of any. The situation certainly looked hopeless, for not much can be done to defend themselves by men who are bound securely, as well as outnumbered. Yet Lord Alfred kept an undaunted front, and gave old answer: "So my suspicions were not unfounded, and both you and the Princess Delilah are as villainous as I suspected you to be! lam continced that your other friend, Prince Carlos of Mitylene, is also in the swim. But take care! The Devil's votaries do not always triumph, and with all your cunning you are not invulnerable." "Neither was Achilles," retorted the Duke, "and it was unfortunate for him that he shewed his heel to the enemy. My condition is something like his, in that I am practically invulnerable when face to face with my—what shall I call them ?—suppose we say clients? And, by way of demonstrating the lesson I have received from Homer's classic example, it is only necessary for. me to mention that my present clients will not enjoy the advantage of attacking my one weak spot. In other words, I mean to keep you in full view until the end of the chapter—your chapter, I mean." There could be no mistaking the significance of this last threat, for there was a diabolical triumph in the Duke's voice, which proved that lie considered himself to be on the verge of complete victory. Yet, strangely enough, both Lord Alfred, Mr. Rizzen-Quicke, and Mr. Raleigh felt themselves deprived of all power to indulge in violent recriminations, and each second that passed demon- strated only too surely the fact that all three were being subjugated by the will of a man who had good reason to .boast that he could control his _ _</p>
        <p>7cnts " by keeping face to face with them. He 'ttld laid a spell upon them, and as his voice first ,-ose and fell in rhythmic waves of sound, and then became. droningly monotonous, all active resistance to their fate departed. Indeed, by the time the hypnotist —for such he was—proceeded to impose his com- mands upon them, • they were as ready to obey him as Joseph Rizzen-Quic6 had been on that former occasion when the accidental falling of a horse had rescued him from a deadly peril. One of the three men who had so unexpectedly received our friends was Prince Carlos himself, and the other was his confidential 'attendant and door- keeper. The man dcwnstairs was in the Duke's personal service. lie, had already beei relieved of his bonds, as well as o/ the gag which impeded his speech, and had already been complimented ttpon thb li with which he had helped to dupe the in- r. they would have searched the house fully, evt, he had not been there; but his presence :Li ~;ene had served a twofold purpose. If occasion n,-ecled, he could exercise a little diplomacy to make sure that the upstairs trap had not been prepared in vain; and his loud moans, when he pretended to hurt himself, were a signal that all was going on well. The strangest thing connected with the present demonstration of hypnotic power was the fact that all three victims should - have been unable to resist it: for each of them possessed the advantage of knowing that the Duke was a man who must not be trusted. - .</p>
        <p>Unfortunately, previous resolves to the contrary notwithstanding, they were enthralled mentally and bodily by his fatal influence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>The Duke, therefore, anticipated very little' difficulty in dealing with his captives. But he. intended to make important use of them before he, finally disposed of them, and his cupidity was not - yet proof against the temptation to enrich himself still further, in spite of the obvious fact that such a proceeding could not fail to be extremely dangerous both to him and his accomplices. Prince Carlos knew very well what was in his mind, and, for the first time during the long partnership_ which had existed between them, he felt it necessary to urge greater cautiousness. "Otto," he whispered, "take care what you are doing with these people. If you will heed my advice, you will not attempt anything dangerous with them, after what has happened before. Itizzen-Quicke is sure to have instructed his busi- ness people to be on their guard against anything irregular in his dealings with them. It will be a. much wiser plan to chair' them as soon as pos- sible; for while they are still alive, our castle of cards may be pulled down about our ears at any moment."</p>
        <p>"What!" sneered the Duke, still keeping his eyes upon the group, of prisoners, "is my friend Carlos turning coward, after shewing himself for years to be one of the most daring schemers in existence? And is he losing all confidence in the scientific skill which has never failed us yet?" "Tush! " was the impatient rejoinder. "It is merely a question of prudence; and my scientific skill shews no signs of failing us, unless you ruin everything through your insatiable greed!" `,`You have never known me to blunder yet."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps not hitherto; but you are on the verge of blundering now." "That is just what your sister said to me this morning. You remember that after she had told us all about last night's farce, in which she masqueraded as Madame Celia, she and I were left alone for a short time while you went to make arrangements/ for the little reception we have just held. No sooner were we left en tete-a-tete than I was subjected to a rigid cross-examination, and was told to beware against continuance in sin. Was there ever anything more absurd ? " "Perhaps the affair is not quite so absurd as you suppose it to be. I happen to know what it is that Delilah is vexed about; and it may be just as well to remind you that she is not a woman to be trifled with."</p>
        <p>"You are either jesting, Carlos, or you do not know what you are talking about. Are you aware that I have been accused of being in love with the Countess Sforza, and that I have been rebuked because my attentions to her were too palpable? I never knew your sister to descend to anything so trivial before; and if I didn't know that she was the most unlikely person in the world to fall in love. herself, I should believe that she was jealous."</p>
        <p>This last was said with such a smile of amuse- ment that it was easy to see how little importance the Duke attached to what he believed to be merely a random shot. He had known the Princess Delilah for years, and during all that time she had played with men's hearts, and had aroused their fiercest passions, without ever betraying any danger of losing her own heart; and it would never have occurred to the Duke that he could by any pos- sibility have been the means of awakening in her the knowledge of what love actually could be. If he had only stopped to remember that he had him- self been struck by one of Cupid's arrows, it would have been easier for him to comprehend the seriousness of the Princess Delilah's jealousy, and he would not have mistaken a Niagara of passion for a placid streamlet of Platonic regard. Even the more than gentle hints of Prince Carlos proved unconvincing; and he clenched the matter for the time being, by remarking impatiently: "Upon my word, you must look upon me as an epitome of vanity, if you expect me to believe that the woman who has known me for twenty years would lose her head over me now.- She is so used to being adored by every man whom she chooses to enslave that she is becoming intolerant of any other woman's fascinations; and it is nothing but this which makes her regard ray admiration for the Countess Sforza as a species of defection against herself. But she will have to reconcile herself to it; for I admit that I at length know what love is myself, and I intend, willy nilly, to marry the lovely Countess ere long." "What! You would marry! You must be</p>
        <p>turning mad!" , "No, only human. But enough of that for the present; and to shew you that I am still as amen- able to reason as ever, I will forego my intention of blackmailing Mr. llizzen-Quicke and Lord Alfred Vavasour-Pomeroy, and will be satisfied with the harvest we have already made this season." With this concession, Prince Carlos tried to be satisfied; and he forthwith concentrated his atten- tion upon immediate arrangements. But he was ill at ease, and could not help wondering what would happen when his sister, who was supposed by the world at large to to be merely a friend of his, dis- covered how serious the Duke's love affair was. He did not take the Countess Sforza's feelings into consideration at all, for he knew that if his hypnotising friend so willed it, the poor lady would not be able to prevent herself from marrying him. As shewing how oddly oblivious people can be of their own want of control and judgment, it is sufficient to mention that the Prince never once thought that his own contemplated secession from the ranks of celibates might prove . as serious in its effects upon the business relations of the triangular partnership as the love affairs of his sister and his friend could be. In a word, all three were more willing to sacrifice the personal desires of the others than to recognise the necessity of controlling their own inclinations. If Lord Alfred and his companions could have known of the under-currents of emotion which were 21reatening to impair as strange a business relation- ship as the world has ever seen, they might have felt some slight hope of their own 'future release from a situation which was all the more unpromising because of the completeness of certain other mysterious disappearances, all of which they now knew to be the work of the men who were convers- ing in too low a tone for them to distinguish what it was they were talking about. That it boded disaster to himself and his companions was a natural conclusion for Lord Alfred to \arrive at. But he was as curiously apathetic as his fellow- unfortunates; and so weirdly strong was the Duke's influence that not one of the prisoners attempted the slightest protest when they were told that they would have to remain where they were for the rest of the day. "We will leave you to your own meditations now," said the Duke sardonically. "The Prince and I have business up in London; but you have no need to feel nervous at being left in a haunted' house, for we shall leave some efficient guardians behind us, who will take care of you until you are removed to your final destination some time to- night." A moment later, the Duke and the Prince were ready to return to town until it was late enough for so large a party of people to leave Hamlet House together without being observed. They had not troubled to go to the agent's office to ask for the keys of the house, but had effected an entrance through one of the dilapidated back windows, with what result we have seen. Yet they neglected no obvious precautions; and they sent one of their men to take back the keys, and to say that his master did not think the house would suit him. This would prevent the agent or his clerks from coming to look after the keys ; and when the arch con- spirators went back to London, they believed themselves to he as certain of victory in the presellt transaction as they had been in all former ones. How far their, confidence was justified remains to, be seen. (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
