        <p>THE BRABA.ZON SCHEME, --- SALE OF WORK OF WARRINTGION</p>
        <p>WORKIIOUSE INAAT ES.</p>
        <p>There was a practical owcome of the intro luction o the Brabazon Scheme into the Warrington U tion Work- house od Tars lay when an exhibition and sale of the work don t by the aged and infirm ihm it,s of the Work- house were held in the Town HAI. The w tiai neon set out in the Council Chamb r, and/t Le visitlrs were agreeably surprised at the excellenc3 of the ?oils dis played. The henli work showed that exisiderahle skiil and care had been takei by the inmate's, ani much t and patience must have been shown b 7 the ladies wn-, have taught them. The articlEs comprised door mats, baskEts, rice, bamboo and reed screens, ornamental metal work, and wearing apparel. lisady purcnasers were found, and the financial result must have been very satisfactory.</p>
        <p>At the opening proneedinge. the Re.ltor (Rlv Canoe Willis) prPsided. Amongst those present wera ti Revs Ef V Pigot, and F E Pnvell ;Mr John Whit J P., Mr Thomas Birchall, J.P., and :he folowing members of the Board of Guardians :—Mr Clav, Mrs ElAs;en, and Messrs A I Jolley, S Davies, FUni :iron. and T B Ashcroft. There was a large attend ince of ladies from the-town and district.</p>
        <p>After a few commendatory rrmarki from tho chairman</p>
        <p>Mrs. EDELSTEN sa d she had received several kind let- ters from friends who were unable to be pre ,erve The Rev: W. LI. Drummond wrote r —4* 20, Bold-street, War- rington, March 20:h, 1899. Dear Mrs. E amn very sorry that I have to go to E linburgh to-rnerr ow, and cannot accordingly bi present at the Brakemen Exhibition. I hope it will be very successful, especially in drawing public attention to the geed work which you and others have been doing am -mg the infirm inmate; ot our workhouse. Ever 9 time I go into our workhouse hospital I come away with a fresh feeling of sympathy for the dreary and stricken lives, maey of whom are there for all that remains to then of tots world. To °ass the dreariness of thsir lot and impart some freshness and variety to their surroundings is the b ,st that we can do for them ; and lam very glad to think that our work- house is not without these helpful and humaniz,ng in- fluences. The words of cordial recognition spoken by Mr Jenner-Fust at the meeting of our Board last week were most gratifying. May the Breese :a work go on and premer !—With kind regards, very sincerely yours, WILLIAM H. Deummoreo." She had also received a note from Mies Dutton with a small contribution to the fund. Mrs. Edelsten then read an account of the Braille an work generally, and of the Warrington branch in particular, so that the audi- ence reight understand the circumarances wider which the work had heen made. The B-abazen Enoloywnt Society she said was founded Li 1833 by the Countese of MPath, then Lady Brabizen. Wtailst visieing at differ- oni; workhouses. the noticed how most of the old and Infirm, and the c,nvalescent in the infirmary, were sit- ting idle and listless all the day long. It oecurrei t 3 her that some fancywork or light employment would help to make the time pass more pleasantly for them. She volunteered, therefore, to pay for materials if ladies , could be found who would give their time to teaching these peorle.l hat was the beginning of the scheme. Ken- sington Workhouse was the first to take it up, and the branch there in the infirmary has continued ever since in the most flourishing conditien. The scheme was no g in operation in no fewer than 178 workhouses. Permis- sion was asked on September 10th, 1898 by the Ledies' Workhouse ViFiting Committee to introd ice the scheme into the Warrington Workhouse Infirmary. This was at first refused, but about a year later the question was again raised, this time by one of the Guar- dians, Mr. George Thompson, and permission was granted, Tuesday afternoons from two to four being the appointed time for the ladies to visit. The ladies began work on October 26:h, 1897, having obtained a loan of £lO from the Central Committee in London, for the purchase of materials. This loan of course had to be gradually repaid. The ladies win volunteered for the work, and whose names were passed by the Guardians, were Mrs E Atkin, Mrs McGregor, Miss Broadbent, Miss Cexon, Miss Dekin, besides Miss Massey and herself on the Ladies' Visiting Committee. She should like to say bow deeply she regretted Missr Massey's absence that day. She acted as secre'Ary fo</p>
        <p>this brancft for some tims, p orchasieg aod givine out the materials, mad hal dev ted heroelf he trt ail s the work. See had left everything in the most perfect order, and she (Mrs. E lelstoe) thouehl ever vone con- ' nectee with tile Brab seen work oueet to feel imm enselv indebted to her. (Appleuse.) Teey bean wih Hix °wells —three weinen and t nese mei. Alteee'll r they bad had nine women pispile and 16 mm these were censtantly chsneing, a d they hsd lost by i-eath one girl aad thr In en. One of these was their best bent-irne work He hid been an iron werker,an I was deigeted to handle tools again. He was dying of caesurae etion. an I she f sure that this occupetiou biehtiene I all cheered' him as Lm eas he wa.4 4.1. c to ao it. (4.po'a ) 0 hers of course, got better and went out. or wire removed to other putts of the wellshouse. They ceuld not grumble at this, altheuele they get fond of their pupils end core sorry when they left them. Besides superintendine the work, the ',Liles rea I or talked to their peel's, an,l very cerdiel relations wire established hst wean thsm One energ,tic lady gave her piopils hiitoriil and geographicel lectures and ksot them up to date in pelitics and geleril news. A. 4 regarded their fineness they had just managed to lease on the right side. See laid their balance sheet top to the end of December b,fore her fellow eau. lions a' few days ago in accordance with the rules of the seci 'tY, and it showed a balance in hand of £2 1 10 Since the end of December they had sold £3 133 6i we-th of werk and had boueht materials to the value el 2 124 %el, thus leevine her roughly with £1 towards the exp -ns?s of that sale, no a large artmanie She was much in- debted to the Mayor for kindly giving her the use of the Town Hall. Mr °lnner also had conveyed the work h nTs They ne7h a hd etn not, table of sr of ooru rtsl eve mtnecah- there from the workhnisse free of charge, ai hncl Mr Ralph room. (Apolaose.) to offer fir sale. P 7 was barely £2O worth. If anyone would be good enough to become an annual subscriber she would be only toe delighted to take their names ; also she should be glad to take orders for rugs and screens. Tame) 77a.51 a development of the Btabs- zoi Employment Seheme, work that see should like to mention. In Manchester, at the Crampsall Werkhoise, thsy had a fund in coenectien with the Pliocene for helping deserving cases when they left the er4rkhooso. The Guardians from time to time recommended anger- entlr. deserving cases for aid from the fund. These wisps, which numbed 74, were carefully considered, and tee help furnished *tenor ling to individual needs. Hawkers' licence; and stocks of smaltwares were pin- chased ; clothing, tolls, and spectacles provided ; articles of clothing yvre redeemed from pawn ; persons were sent for three wieilts each to a seaside c mvalescent hospital, and the maintenance of ROVOnti infants temporarily undertaken to allow the yoeni mothers an opportunity of getting a vitiation outide the Work- , house, and of thus being pleceil in a pesitiee ti subse- qinseely support their ebildree hr theft own hnnolarable efforts, New that was her amhitien, for the Warrington branch to be able to give ah&amp; pin hand to tense urd or- tunlte persons who, after long sickness, perhaps left the Workhouse in a practically penniless condition. (Hear, hear.) The Geardians were oftee appealed to for help by such persons, but of course could net eive anything out of the rates. She hop she had convincd them that the scheme was doing a good work, eimple and unnretentious. Its only aim was to help the poor and suffering inreatis of our Workhoeses, to brighten their otherwise dull and monotonous lives and to comfort their declining years. The motto of the society had been well chosen; it. was " Love aid serve," and in this spirit they endeavoured to carry on their work. (Ap- plause). -</p>
        <p>The 'CHAIRMAN Clid t'-ev had lintenel to an interestinv. and touching report. Thal, knew what a depp inter,st Mrs. Edelsten to in the wlrlr. Shl had been far horn well, and it was mtv:h to her credit that she h.d devoted so much time and labour to it.</p>
        <p>Mn. LYON said that to see so many kind frienda made her somewhat nervous, but at the same time, she had muoh plevure in ftecla!iug the Blie opin, the object nf which mu,:t app ,al to all of tluem,as it was to endeavour to brighten the lives of tho7ts who were less happily placel thao themselves. (Applause.) Mr. A.J. Jomay,in proposing a ylte of thanks to Mrs. Lyon for her service,, sii I reference hal been made to the fad that whin it was firit proposed to introduce the scheme at the Workhouse the proposition waske-</p>
        <p>jected by th Ghardiahs. It would be idle to deny that for some time mere was a feeling of hestility, or At least of hesitation, in the minds of some of tae members and lon tae part of $3t113 Of the officiala, as to the danger 4 th it might result from the intradaction oE the ccheine. Thera was notne lila° element of danger and risk in introducing voluntary work into a public in- stitutha. Re was not MO of those who shared the feeling of betitati en, and from the first he championed the introduct;oa of the scene, Ion; he reoognise 1 that his colleagues Iffcrel fro n him sinvrely and honestly, but any one vi Ain the hue ,eeing the inmates at word, and also seeing the work in that reom, could no longer doub; and all hesitati,n had been swept away by reaults which rllected great credit on all conceraed in theyork. _(Applause.)</p>
        <p>L'Ar. S. DAVIE 3 seconded the vote of thanka. He might venture s4v th It even many members of the Board had not the slightest idea that so much work had heel done. Great praise was certainly due to Mrs. E le'steh, Miss Massey, aid others. (Hears, hear.) Mr. WILLIAM HOUGIITON was pieaTel of the oppor- tunity to show nis appreciation of whet had been done during the 12 or 18 mon.hs .the Brabazon scheme had been in operation, and to 09 ab:e to explain his position. When the subject was first introduced open hostility was displa.vPd by a section of the Board ; others viewed it with indiff vanes, and a third section displayed great enthusiasm in it. For himself he could net see, as he now saw, the good work that was likely to result. At first he must classify himself as belonging to the in- different sectioh, as he thought the scheme might inter- fere with the discipline of the house. fie Gould not therefore give it much encouragement, but after what they h Id seen that day he believel the Guardians would be unanimous in supporting the scheme. (Hear, bear, and applause.) The sale of goods then commenced.</p>
