      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CFICTRCRES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CFICTRCRES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The meeting of this society on Sunday afternoon in Wycliffe Church was very satisfactory. Mr. Thomas Wallington, one of the hon secretaries of the society, presided, and made a few remarks wii h respect to tne chairmanship of the meetings The speaker for the afternoon was Mr. Harvey, of Widnes, who deliveied a, wry' thoughtful and instructive address on "Christian- ity and Politics." Ile showed the relation of the one with the other, and the necessity for Christian men to exert what influence they had in public affairs fcr the common good. He condemned Christian people for their apathy in public work and said many of the evils of the present day would not be remove" until Christian men and women brovght the principles of Christ to bear upon them. Miss Senior was the soloist and gave a fine in- terpretation of "0 Hest in the Lord." The service was also taken part in by Mr. B. Farrar, and Mr. Robins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>"CHRIST AND SOCIAL DUTY: OUR OBLIGATIONS."</head>
        <p>"CHRIST AND SOCIAL DUTY: OUR OBLIGATIONS."</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon, in St. James' C urch, Latch- ford, the Rev. F. E. Powell tock for his subject "Chii it and Social Duty i Our obligations." He observed that the words debt and duty had a common origin. When they fulfilled their duty they were paying their debts. They were all under obligations the one to the oth‘r. But how apt were they to dwell upon heir rig,hts,how slow to recognise their duties. If workers, instead of always dwelling upon the rapacity of employers, wouli think of their obligations ;if employers instead of always em- phasizing the selfishness of the men wou'd think more of their duty to them, how different their industrial ques- tions would appear. The principle of social duty was implied in every purchase we effect. They should, one and all, try to discourage those forms of trade, or any customs in a trade, which led to the sweating cr degradation of the workers. The question was not " How little need I pay ?" but " What ought I to pay ?" They ought to feel uncomfortable and unhappy if they found that something they had bought cheaply had been produced at the cost of some poor man's or woman's or child's suffering. Social duty also involved them dealing with everything that concerned the well- being of the community—sanitation, proper hsiise accommodation, the better centre( of the liquor traffic, and urged them to secure such conditions for all that all might live healthy, happy and decent lives. Every man and every woman ought to have a voice in the making of the laws by 'which they were b)und. Hours of labour, which in many cases were cruelly excessive, ought to be shortened, so that people.) might learn to live human lives, rather than to erci!it as mere lab eur machines. Child labour should be limited ; the condi- tions of female labour improved ; the poor better housed ; a living wage and regularity of employment secured so far as possible, and in old age a generous pension provided There was wealth in the country to provide aa increa-a of armaments because these were thought to be ni ceseary. Fiqualy necessary,—nay a thousand old more necessary, was to see to the physi- cal, mental and moral well-being of the community, the navy exists to defend.</p>
        <p>SAD DEATH OF A GRA.PPENHALL FARMER</p>
        <p>On Monday morning, at the Parr Arms, Grappenhall, Mr. Yates, coroner, held an injuiry into the circum- stances attending the death of Altred Barker, 54 years of age, farmer, who resided at Whitehouse Farm, Grappen- hall.—Mrs. Sarah Barker, wife, said her hasb and got up on Saturday mornlug at 5.10, and went out of the house without having anything to eat. At 630 she went to the shippon, where he had been milking, and told him to go for his breakfast. He did so, but never complained about being unwell. He wag anxious about getting the threshing done,' as some of the men did not turn up. He helped to feed the machiae from 7.30 until 7.40, and then said he would go to see if he could eet another man to help them. Deceased came in again later and went cut and witness looking through the window shortly after saw him lying on his back in the yard. He was unconscious when she got to him and died before h doctor arr.vet Her husband had an attack of inflaeoza six years ago and it had left its effects upon him. He was a very nervous man.—Richard Reid, of La mm, said he was driver of the thrashing machine at Mrs. Barker's. De- ceased had been helping but had not over-exqrted self. When deceased came to him at the engiue he loakt d different than a few minutes bef( re. He toil sitD.sss he was going into the house for a few minutes as he could hardly get his breath. A In ry short time after, Mrs Barker called to witness, and on going to see what was the matter, he feud Mr. Barker on his back gasping for breath.— The Coroner said that dreaded complaint influenza left something behind in 99 cases out of every hundred. He suggested that deceased had a weak heart and with com- mencing work cn an empty stomalh, an at•mk of syn- cone had been brought on.—The jury retuned a verdict of death from ss.ncope caused by over-exertion, and ex- pressed their sincere sympathy with the widow and family of the deceased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>GETHSEMANE.</head>
        <p>GETHSEMANE.</p>
        <p>" And He was withdrawn from them about a stone's oast, and kneeled down, and prayed, saying. Father, if Thou be willing, remove this cup from me ; nevertheless not my will. but Thine be done And being in an agoa He prayed more earnestly ; and His sweat was as it were great drops of blood falling to the ground. And when He rose up from prayer, and was come to His diiies, ho found then sleeping for sorrow."—Lulce xth 41, 42, 44, 45.</p>
        <p>The paschal hymn had died away, Deserted was the...!' upp r room " ; And darkness on Gethseln'ne lay, When Jesus sought its shelt'ring gloom. His lowly frie: d 3 o'erc nue with sk‘ep, They all had striven t 3 contra!, Watch with their Master could not keep, Nor share the anguish of His sou!, More bmeath the olives' shade, The Man of sorrous meekly knelt; And there in agony he Prayed, But who may tell the pangs he feltj! No human eye beheld film there, None saw His blo3d-3weat trickling slow ; Anr4 heavers alone could hear His prayer That sprang from more than mortal woe. Ail. wherefore r0.,2 His pleading cry, Why fell those droos of crimson hue ? Say, aid the' sinlsss fear to die, Era yet th.! Cross had naq His view. 0 not from death did .TNiusEhtink, For he had power to rise again : 'Twas His another Cup to drink That had no type in hurral pain. In that dark hour the tempter tried His spot-lers nature to enthral ; And all his arts infernal plied, If itap;y he might see Him fall. But all in vain diu Satan wield His weapons or that fell design ; The power of God was there to shicig, For Jesus was Himself divine. 0 had He failed in that dread strife To drink the cup His Fith,l. gave; Men bad not known the way of life, Nor found a rans,m from the grave. J. H. W. CLINDINNING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
