      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>TUE EX AIIIN</head>
        <p>TUE EX AIIIN</p>
        <p>MARCH 24th 1899.</p>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT of the Is there no remedy for Board of Trade and Trades Trade Conflicts ? 'Unions have been anxious to co-operate with the Employers' Parliamentary Council in forming a national board of conciliation. The employers, however, " do not at the present time see their way to the possible formation of a full and satisfactory body of employers and employed on a board of conciliation,"as suggested, and so the project has been dropped. This is a Matter for general regret. The reasons for the failure are not far to seek. The masters think the are now in a better position to make war 11Pon trades unions than they ever were before, 44(1 they have just, as far as one branch of trade is e°neerned, embarked upon a new conflict with that end in view. The current tendency is decidedly towards these largo conflicts, and it is due to the Power which federation gives. The pros- Peet of winning victories on one side Or the other is too alluring to permit of the free play of common sense. There would seem to be no local and general remedy, except in very few cases. Local boards of conciliation Work very well, and wherever masters and men C 551 be induced to sit round the same table and talk over business matters good results are highly Pr°hable. But we may be sure of this, that if we are entering upon an era of fresh conflict the ques- tion which heads thihrticle will most certainly be asked. The pessimists will not admit there is a remedy. Some of them say that as long as trades Unionism exists there will be conflicts, and that their dissolution is the condition precedent to in- dustrial peace. Industrial bargains and lower wages would follow. As the men would be quite unable to struggle against the master3,there would be industrial peace. Another view is that, whilst the two combatants must be organisal, some vol- Aintary arrangement is the utmost paint to which We can aspire. Mr. RITCHIE holds this view, and it is 'pretty general. But it does no Carry us very far. It did not prevent the en- gineers' trouble, nor has it avertel the plasterers' 100k-out. Trials of strength have been proceed- ing all the same. Is there no other remedy within the reach of a statesman's arm? That there is not is the accepted opinion. Themornent anyone casations compulsory arbitration it is almost as if there had been an explosion of dynamite. Toe 18W persons who advocate this common-sense znethod of making the whole react upon its parts 'the true principal of all legislation—are always told that neither side would accept it, that awards could not be enforced, and that great mischief Would ensue. The alternative, of course, is to , bear the ills we have. But the amount of patience in doing so is very small. Public opinion is always being appealed to by one side or the other, and yet they only allow it a moral force. What is Wanted is to get some other forco—the force of enlightened law.</p>
        <p>New Aftican THE new agreement between A Settlement. Franc 3 and England has a pre-</p>
        <p>sent and a future bearing. It nieans a settlement of the outstanding difficulties about the Bahr-el-Ghazal and Like Chad, but it 4130 means that someday French influence will ex- tend over the whole of North-western Africa, .Ir°ln Wadai to Algeria, and from the south of the 'ibYan Desert to the Atlantic. For England it Tiseans that we shall never evacuate Egypt, and at We shall consolidate our power from Cairo to ..,.'ganda, and from the Nile westward through Js-ordofan and Darfur, and from Darfur southward bithe French Congo to the boundaries of the Bel- gian Congo, and then to the Nile east- Ward. It will be a very, big settlement indeed, relieving France from many anxieties, this 01.intry from chances of conflict west of the but very materially increasing our suscep- tibility to what may be done in Abyssinia. If, with Russia established in Abyssinia, and France hi Wadai and Dar Banda, any serious difficulties were to occur we should be in danger of getting lipped severely just where the Nile begins to issile from the Great Lakes. No doubt the settle- lent increases the value of the Nile valley as a nleans of communica4ion between Egypt, the Bcludan, and Central and Southern Africa. Lord ALISBURY having arranged -this agreement by keans of a convention, and gone to the South of larance,Mr. RHODES reappears on the scene to take Part in another deal,in which the KAISER and Mr. "lAMBERIAIN have to play a very considerable Pt. It cannot be said that the French settle- bent is unfair, or that it gives away anything to 'which we were seriously entitled. It will 11doubtedly give to France an outlet to " Nile, which was one of the objects °f the various missions of which that 11Iajor MARCHAND has attracted most attention. the other hand, it should also give us from the 'Lllger territories access to Lake Chad, and from thence, if desirable, to the Nile. The advantages 4t re thus of a mutual character, though, if we ob- -441 more territory, we are likely to have fewer e4tnniercial advantages. But it was time some wile.ttlement were made; and probably the Frenc ,111 regard the arrangement as giving them con- iliderably more than they could have expected, and</p>
        <p>as handing over to them some districts in whieh they have not yet established any kind of authority. The plan has the disadvantage of making it im- perative that we should assert a military authority in Kordofan, where the KIIALIFi is still in power, and in Darfur, to which we have not hitherto directed any attention whatever. The sooner the two steps are taken the better. It is very pro- bable that the autumn will not be allowed to pass without efforts to dislodge, beat, and disperse the KFIALIFA'S new force, and when this is done some expedition will have to be sent into Darfur. What this may mean we cannot tell ; and the ad- visers of the Egyptian Government will have to give the matter their best attention.</p>
        <p>Our American cousins have a liking for improv- ing on some of the old customs and practices of the Mother Country. In many instances their ingenuity and enterprise is turned to good account, but we cannot say that we look with favour upon the American mode of carrying out the capital sentence. It is novel—like most things from across ,the water—and it is in accordance with the highest branches of scientific knowledge, but electrocution is not an innovation that we look favourably upon. This week has seen the first execution of a female criminal by electricity, the operation being performed at Sing Sing prison "with neatness and despatch." Mrs. Place was a murderess of a particularly brutal type and fully deserved the severest penalty of the law, but it does not accord with one's sentiments and feelings that she should be done to death in this up-to-date fashion. Electrocution involves the shaving of the criminal's head and the baring of a leg for the application of the electrode, and it is this scienti- fic detail that repels one most, though the execu- tion itself may be quite painless. In the case of a woman public sympathy is greater, but in any case it is not at all pleasant to contemplate the killing of human beings after the cold-blooded lmanner:in which stray dogs are destroyed in the ethal chamber. It is probable that public ooinion will compel the American authorities to abolish electrocution, and thie will be good news, even if the old plan of hanging by the neck until dead be reverted to.</p>
        <p>Some of the French papers seek to satisfy the insatiable craving of their readers for excitement by printing at more or less inopportune times articles which are supposed to prove how ridicu- lously easy a thing it would be for France to invade England. Frenchmen in by-gone generations ever found this task impossible of performance but their successors appear to have no doubts as to their ability to succeed. The "Revue Des Deux Mondes in a recent issue had an article on the "Invasion of England" embodying one of the most hare-brained, nonsensical schemes we ever had the good fortune to laugh at. Fifteen thousand steam barges are to be built, armed, and filled with men and horses on the various rivers and canals of France and then, starting simul- taneously across the Channel, they are to land 170,000 men, minus 10,000 killed by our volunteers, in England. The rest is easy. Each barge is to carry a quick-firing gun and a torpedo, and the latter is to sink any British man- of-war which dares to approach. At the same time Ireland is to be armed with the hundreds of thousands of old Gras rifles now rusting in French arsenals. We are tempted to ask, in the words of the belligerent schoolboy," What would we be doing P" while all this was happening. But the idea is too ridiculous for serious contemplation, and the minds of Frenchmen must indeed be strange organs if they can absorb such inflated theories as this.</p>
        <p>It is perhaps too mach to hope that further re- flection will convince Mr. Walter Long of the reasonableness of the demand on the part of the public that purchasers of tinned milk should know exactly what they are buying. No one can deny that condensed milk is popularly supposed to be suitable for infants and invalids. Thousand of tins are sold for infants and, as medical evidence shows, many of them are practically only chalk and water, the result being in some places an infant death-rate of 40 per cent. The pur- chasers cannot be expected to know all about this. They pay over their money in the belief that in exchange they will obtain an article that will strengthen the body and please the pal- ate. The President of the Board of Agriculture says that to insist upon labels that would protect consumers against their misplaced confidence would be to throw too great a burden upon the Government. In that case the margarine lakel must throw too great a burden upon the inspect- ing authority; but they have never complained of it, and we do not imagine that a descriptive label upon condensed milk tins would be heavier. Yet, if that were so, time and money are n)t to be con- sidered when it is a question of saving forty lives out of a hundred for the benefit ot the State and the household.</p>
        <p>Cromwell, instead of Horatius, is to guard the bridge —Warrington bridge of course. The General Purposes Committee met last week and decided that the most suitable site for the handsome statue given by Mr. Monks—a pictorial presentment of which will be found on another page—would be outside the Old Academy at Bridge Foot. The GeneralParpose3 Committee consists of the whole of the members of the council, so it is reasonable to suppose that their decision will be ratified when it comes before the Council proper at its next meeting. We are not prepared to vouch for the certainty of Cromwell's installation on this site, for some strange things happen at times between a decision in committee and' its for- mal ratification, but we think that the suitability of position is such that it must find favour with the majority. For reasons romantic and historical the great Protector's statue cou,ld not be placed on a better site, and then again what a handsome ap- proach to the town it will make when the bridge gets widened, as it will, eitherin our time or somebody else's.</p>
        <p>EASTER HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>CYCLES ! CYCLES!! CYCLES !! !</p>
