      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RIINCDRN FREE LIBRARY' COMHITITE.</head>
        <p>RIINCDRN FREE LIBRARY' COMHITITE.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the Runcorn Free Library Committee was held on Tuesday evening and was presided over by Mr. Daniel Bisbrown. Toe Libra- rian (Mr. J. Dlones) rep)rted during the month of Feb- ruary that the institatioa was opened 20 days. The total number of volumes lent out was 1,931, a &amp;cream of 171 on the correspondi,ig period last year. The subjects embraced were :—Theology, 28 ; philosophy 10 ; science, 67 ; history. 114 ; poe'rv. 12 ; fic ' 7,ion 1,463 ; miscel- laneous, 246; French, 6: Itttlile, 12. Library repots were reaeived from Widnes and Fulham. Sir John Brunner' Bart., M.P., had presente 1 26 voluer es of Natural History, in accordanee with the pro miss made by him at tne reopening of the Institation a snort time ago. Mr. Charles Tomkins had presented a copy of "'To-day." Voss of thanks were accor led the donors and special rneatioa of the commitlee's indebtedness to Sir John Brunner was made. MILTON P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual meeting of the Milton P.S. Ea. was held on Sunday, when there was an excellent attendance. Toe chair was taksn by one of the Vice-Presidents (Nlr. G. Lacey), who gave a capitat five minutss' address on " Drsams," welch was highly appreciated. The solo " Behold I stand at the doar and knock" was admirably sung by Mr. Gowanlock, of Rancqrn and was well received. The President (Rev. J. Pedlar) gave the ad- dress, and spoke a few words of encouragenvatto the [l3 took as his subject " Dead to sin," showing how a mah to ty benoml so accustomed to a Vice as to be totally uncoudcious Of the act of sinning, or oblivious to the fact tha7. he was committing a violation of the law. The address was very good; and was well ap- plied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>SIIICIDIS AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>SIIICIDIS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon, at the Adelphi Hotel, Widnes. Mr.F. A. Jones, deputy-coroner, held an inquest on the b)dy of Robert Bamber, aced 54 years, labourar,Walms- leF-street,who was band in a pit near Tanhouse,Widnes- lane, on Monday night. It appeared from the evidence that the deceased, who had twice been in an asylum, had suffered from a severe cold for several weeks, and had been strange in his maon3r. On Monday evening he had tea about half-ptst six. He then remarked to his wife that something was wrosg.and he would either have to b 3 killed or drowned. He went out shortly afterwards, and as he did not return a search was in- Eritnted, and his body was found in a pit of watsr. He was at once got out, and as he appeared to breathe sligh:ly artificial respiration was res)rre-i to for about throc-quartert of an hour, but without avail. 'The jury brougnt it in a verdict of "Suicide while of unsound mi4d."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S P.S. EVKNINGS.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S P.S. EVKNINGS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening, in as; Parociaint Rooms, Widnes a large and enthusiastic audience was presided over by Mr J Lunt, who was supporta Iby the Rev J Adams, M.A., and Messrs 11 Taylor andJ Williams. The spPcial 'speaker vrae Mea Taylor, Police Cou,t Missionary, War- rington. He took as his subj.ct. "Public house Signs and their meanings," and his remarks were both in. structive and amusing. An excellent programme was supplied by Mr Fairclough, and encores were frecieent'y demanded. The first musical item was a glee, " Men of Harlech," sung by the party, and followed by a sou, "The Skipper," by Mr T N Johnson. Miss Timperley tastefully rendered," She were a wreath of roses," and the duet, "The Spider and the-Fly," by Miss Hethering- ton and Mr 11 Johnson, was heartily applaudel Mips Hetherington also sang "The Year glides by." Mr Wesley 0 Cooke was very euncsssful in his vocal efforts, My voice is near me" and "The Oherister," The musical debate," Popton Parish Council." given by the Party, was very amusing. Mr W Seal; and Mr J Hough- ton also received the applause of the audience for their solos. Mr R Josnsen, au i Mr S Yates actsd as the accompanists.</p>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEA:MN</p>
        <p>The weekly concert given at this Institute oa Taellay night surpassed any given there for s'nn3 tine. Mr. G. e Hewitt presided over a large ettenden es. Toe whol of the programme. with the exeeption of ele uomer, was submitted by Miss Jukes and her pupils, assisted by Mr. Morris and Mice WOOL And speaking generally a mere finished peefermance than that of the juvenile;, especi- ally Miss Lilly e Silaw, who by tho sin and danced way superbly, weu,d nave done credit to older and more ex• perieteced artistes. Lieutenant Tucker of the schooner " Travails," gave a fiao renderine of" The Skipper and his bey," by reqeest. The renasender of the nroeranoms wae,equally good.—Before the close Mr. J. K. Digges proposed, ani Mr. J. W. Weston rice indefl a sere of thanks t 3 Miss Jukes and her pupils which was ;accorded with acclamation, Ise also was a similar vete to toe chairman proposed by Mr. W. S,IAW, and secondel by Captain Heasley. Toe singing of the National Anthem brought to a close one of the most enj )yable of evening entertainments, and all were loud in praise of tee clever pupils of Miss ASSAULTING A MOTIIER AT RUNC)I2N. On Wednesday, before Messrs. G. L. Davies and F. J. Norman,Joseph Rowena, a young man,was charged on a Warrant with assaulting his mend 'nether, Alice Rewson, of St. John-street, Runcern.—The neether said at neon on the 17th hest, her son came home to' dinner ane with- out having the least provocation struck her a violent blow on toe arm causing her arm OM very black. She described t the magistrates he trouble she had had with the young man in the dock. He weuld not keep his work when he had any. Ho had threatened and assaulted her on many occasions. Mr. Johnson stated that when witness ApAied for tee warrant she said "If her son was sent to prison it would be a bad thing for the people in John-street as be would kill half of them."—The woman now denied this.—P.C. Carrington said he went frequently past the house and heard the prisoner cursing and threatening his neither on numerous occasions. The prisoner was in the habit of sitting in the house brooding and appeared to ina igine that the women in the street were leagued against him. When this idea Came into hie he id he would go out into the street and strike the first womae that came in his way. It was only the other day that he struck a wfmen named Edgececk in the fee?, and gave her a " lovely black eye." Prisoner said he had now got good work at" Dak c "and if the naseeistra'les would let him off, he would sick to it and maintain his mother.—The magistrates thought a month's ruminat- ng at Knutsford with hard labour tiro ffni in woul I be he most beneficial to the young man's health ; and at be same time sent a direction to the Medical Officer of he Prison tokerp the prisoner under observation. 00A.GULINH. —TRANdrAIDINT DENHNE for broken articles.--Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 24, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 24, 1899.</p>
        <p>Si. JOHN AMBULANCE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the local St. Jona Ambulance class, held at tin Technical Insti:ate, oa We inesday evening there wss a fair att;endance. The catir was occupied by Mr. Hutton. To fullowiaz officials were appuinted for the coming session :—President, Mr. E. imison : secretary. Mr. Worrall ; treasurer, Mr. Bakes ; '.l3M- mittee: Messrs Flut:on, Edward.,lihs.w, Astoa, Newton, Inglesfield, Matthews. Unsworts, Welsh, J. D. Jones,and Trßvitt. Dr. F. McDougall is the popular instructor, and the classes will be held in due course.</p>
        <p>RIINCOW:4 PHICHA RAO= SOCIETY.—FINAL CONCERT. The Drill Hall, Runcorn, vrasoccupied by the Runcorn Philharmonic Society, on Thursday evening, when tee third and last coecert of the season was submitted in the usual creditable manner. Tne.chief musical work was Gaunoi's Muse Soleanelle "ad th 3 society's mem- bers showed conzsiderable taste and ability throughout. The chief artists were His A.da Standen, soprano ; Mr Tom Barlow, tenor; and Me. Ernest Ryalls, bass ; all singers of ability. rho Runcorn Orchestral Society per- formed efficiently. Owing to going to press before the conclusion of the concerkwa are unable to give our'usual detailed report. DEATH AND FUNERAL OF A SAILOR.</p>
        <p>Whee the scheouer "Emily and Mary" of Runcorn, arrived in Runcorn on Tuosday, March 14. h, John Roberts (mate) was reported to be lying ill on board. He was visited by Mr.W.Sbaw,missionary,and as Roberts had no home, Mr. Seam undertook to take him to hos- pital. The man gratefully accepted the offer and the following Deeming Mr. Shaw took him off to the Sea- men's Institute, Hanover-streit, Liverpool, and subse- quently to the Southern Hospital through tee intro- mentality of the Rev. E. Lambert. chaplain. Up to Thursday afternoon Roberts seemed ta be doing well,but took a change for the worst about teat time,and passed away in the presence of the doctor and nun°. Heart disease was stated to be the Clll3B of death. Through the kiadaess of Mr. John Meadoweroft, loaal neat of the M.S.C. Co., the body was conveyed to Runcorn in one of the Company's flats, and was takaa to the Sea- men's Institute on Monday night. Oa Tuesday up veer le of 40 sailors, captains, seamen and watermen, assembled at the Institute to show their last respect to theirdeparted comrade, who was borne to his last resting place amidst manifestations of heartfelt sympathy. Tne first part of the service was read at the lastttaca and concluded at the graveside. Tao officiating clergymen being the R,r;r. E. Lambert, senior chaplain. Wreaths were sent by the Capaiu of the vessel evii;le wnom R )berts ha I atom ter about three year, and also by a large numbor of sailors amongst whom Roberts was well kaown and lo'ghly re- spected,</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>EBY&amp; COR aBSPaNDENT .1</p>
        <p>Considerable sympathy prevails with the lament- ing relatives of 01r. Joseph Jackson, who through mental depression, brouy,ht about his untitnaly end on Slonday last. The evidence adducetl before the Coroner disclosed a truly pithetic affe,ltioa of one man for snottier; sad illustrated how warmly at- tached the two men Jackson and Arthur Williams ntust have been. Both men were loyal sous of Rua- corn, highly respected, and generAl favourites ; but that their affections were so closely knitted together as to fill one with the dec,ermination to overcome the barrier of death itself is so touchingly strange that its contemplation overwhelms us.</p>
        <p>The conviction by the Runcorn Bench for drunkenness on licensed premises at Halton shows the determination of the Bench to put down of- fences against the licensing laws. The facts of the case show Us that the house has been used as a place of assembly for the Irish labourer—who it was stated "had nowhere else to go, only to such places" and again "it was a common practice," so the advocate said, " for these Irishmen to so assem- ble all over the country." If this is true, and have no reasonable ground on which to dispute it, I think some measures should be enacted by Parliament to wipe a large and awful blot off the garment of "Christian England." It is high time to decide whether the churchjor the "pub" should be shut.</p>
        <p>The weather which has prevailed lanerly has been phenomenal. Monday, the first day of Spring, saw a heavy downfall of snow, but by the afternom of the stifle day much of it had disappeared under the influence of a powerful sun. Monday evening, however, brought sharp fro3t and the roidways in many placas were rendered extrem3ly dAngeraus both to passengars and vehicular trill-E.. If the prasent condition of things atmospherical prevails the cup ties of Saturday will in ram cases nave to be postponed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Runcorn's C119.11C93 against Leigh are ths subject of mueli speculation, and already the ardent ones are looking forward to an encounter witn Batley— the embolden. All I can say is that Rancorn on form can beat almost any team—and provided they play in the spirit they have d3ne recently I have no doubt the local photographers will have another picture to display. It ramaini to- be seen how they will fare at Leigh, but if natidence c3unts for any- thing in cup-ties then the Linnets s'aould pull through serenely.</p>
        <p>A. 9 the day of the polling draws nearer a little mere spirit is being infused into the two local con- tests for honours as ratepayers' representatives. The candidates seem very hopefal, but how both sides can have the majority they imagine they have—per canvass books of conria—is surprising. But the question will b settlel by next week, and I shall not be at all surprised if the unexpected happens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>SEQUEL TO TKE MORPETS DOCK</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO TKE MORPETS DOCK</p>
        <p>DISASTE It.</p>
        <p>A. DEPRESSED MIN COMIEIN SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Won; ley. corontr, held an inqusit at the Loadon and North-West;ern Raslway Hotel, Runcorn, on Tuesday afternoon, on the by of Mr. Joseph Jack- son, 12, Cinal-street, Runcorn, wh3 V 713 found hanginz by a rope frou a ho3k,in an uo.t sirs roma of his house, on Monday morning about half-past ten o'clock, by his wife.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Emma Jaction sail the deceased 0713 her hue- band, and residsd at 12, Canal:street. He V 7 ig 52 years of age and a flatman. about seYela o'cloc'r on 51 mrily morning he got up anl had hi breakfast. at which time he seemed q lite right. About ten o'clock she re: quested him to go into tee front room whilst she washed the breakfast things. Subsequently from what her daughter told her she went upstairs and found her hus- band hanging by a rope suspenled from a hook. She had previously made a fire in the sitting roo:n for her husband, and sent her daughter up to clean tee hearth. When her (laughter Cial.'2 down she asked her " W father there ?" She said "Ni." It was then she went upstairs and fsunl her husbanA hanging. She sent for the doctor, but it was 90i719 time before the dortor came. Constable) Gregory was first to come. Her husband had been ill for the last nine weeks. He hal been ha, in his mind and the doctor put mental dePression on his club 130t3. She thought it was the Morpeth Ducks disaster that uo- set him, for ia that disaster a young man named ArApir Williams, svhe.had worked wi!sh him fo: the last 121 years, and of whom deceastd was very fond, was drowned. _</p>
        <p>Miss Eva Jaeicsfol corroborated Err mother's state- meat. Se did no see the b3dy hanging as her m )thee asked her to kiep downstairs. There was n 3 one in the house except the mother and father and daughter. Ole of th 3 jurymen said it was al o!l-fashiened house with three storeys and he belicvlii Mr. Jackson used to go up to that room for the purpose of seeing the traffl3 on the canal, as it had a commanding view of the canal.</p>
        <p>Police-Constable Gregory stated he went b the house and took the deceased down. He was warm but quite dead. anl his heart had stopped beating. Afterwar is D. Robinson came in and said Mr. Jackson must have beea dead when taken down. It would be abut 10 minutes past elevea when the doctor earns. Witness knew Jackion well. Frans the atata of the roam he should think that deceased had colloc:ed a bundle of old clothes upon which he stood, and when he had adjusted the rope he must have kicked them away. The Coroner, in summin.c,, up, said it seemlii that an honastoespectable mart wnilst suffering from denressiqh of mini had committed suicide. Upon reviewing the evidence, he could but recommend the jury to bring in a verdict " that the decelsod met his death.by baning; himself at Runcorn, on the 20ta of March, whilst of un- sound mind."</p>
        <p>Th Foreman: That is the c.r.nefal opinioa sir ; there was no more decent man in Runcorn, and he was very highly reaDectid. Daring his lifetime the deceased was connected with the Birkenheal trade. -</p>
        <p>Toroughout the town grett empathy is expreesed with the widow and daulntet and other relatives of the deceased, and his lois is grestly felt amonot a large circle of friends.</p>
        <p>WESTON,</p>
        <p>'ACTT:MIL-AM interesting and highly instructive lecture WAq delivered to a large and atteatire audience by Mr. F. J. Narrbari, LP., on Tuead iv evening, at the Wehleyan schoolroom, Weston. The subject was "Rome" and,the lecture was beautifully Was:rated with lime- light views, which ware tt3r)wn on the screen by Mr. T. Bowpr, Runcorn. Befona the close votes of thanks wfAn passed to the Le3turer and t) the Chairman (Mr. R. P. A.'len) on the prop)sition of Mr. W. Dumbavin, seconded by the Rev. J. A. B. Milvern. The proceeds were in aid of the ladies' sewing class held in connection with the chapel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN CHARACTER SKETCHES. BY 6' Q E.F.D." No. 5.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CHARACTER SKETCHES. BY 6' Q E.F.D." No. 5.</p>
        <p>It would be most unfortunate if this series of sketches were to close without dwelling on the ' characters of at least one or two of those who are true R,uncornians. That is to say persons who have been bred and born lathe town and who justly feel an intense interest in it. not merely from the sordid standpoint of finance, but from the fact that it is the nursery in which they spent their youthful days. To such persons every street, every plot of land and humble cot bring back pleasing trains of thought and endearing recollections of an all two-vivid past bind them to the town and make them feel a glow of patriotism which it would be vain to subdue. It is satisfac;ory to note that there is at least one person who for this reason,takes a fatherly interest in the welfare of Runcorn. Not only is it his proud boast that heris a typical " red-tail " but he is equally proud of the tact that he is an out-and-out Cheshireman. He has good reason to be proud of the progress which he has made, while others who commenced life with equal prospects were content to remain in the same position. He has ever been on the watch for the "tide which taken at the flood leads on to fortune." The consequence is that he has attained a position which cemmande the respect of all; it is a marked advance on his earlier occupation' and adds considerably to his credit. Though not a post which calls for any special exhibition of intellectual power, it is none the less a post which calls for a large amount of more than average ability. The dual capacity in which he serves as a public official is fulfilled in every way satisfactorily. It is not too much to assert that he is one of the most familiar figures in Runcorn. The rich and the poor, the foot and the sage, claim his acquaintance, lie is often reviled; be is often commended. Toe thin,spare figure with hair and beard tinged with grey,together with other signs denote that the winter of age is setting in, and makes him noticeable to all when on his round from" door to door."</p>
        <p>Trio characteristics which distinguish him from other men are few but they are none the less im- portant.He is perhaps a perfect example of the cold- blooded Englishman. It is only when some topic is broached in which he takes an extreme interest that he exhibits any warmth. By nature he is somewhat of a cynic, and the times are few indeed when he fails to betray that sarcasm which pre- vades his whole being. Perhaps he carries this trait a little too often into practice—snubbing per- sons to whom a ward of encouragement should be given rather than a covert sneer. Yet it is a note- worthy fact taut when any vulnerable point happens in spite of the armour of cynicism in which he is enclosed to be pierced by the weapon of an opponent, he invariably makes the mistake of losing his temper. And as this happens to be of a most fiery description; the consequences which follow upon such an out- burst are not always of a pleasant character. He is an old mixture of optimism and pessimism. Shadow and sunshine alternate in his life. This however, may not be noticeable to the outsider for be cannot be said to be a person who wears his keart upon his sleeve, theretore he does not r-flect the passing phases of the inner life to the curious.He holds the most advanced views, and is conse- quently an extremist. Half measures are to him distasteful, and savour to him much of the pusillani- mous. This applies with equal emphasis to 53cia1, I political and religious questions. It is sufficient to observe that he is a gadical of the Labouchere stamp,and a Nonconformist of the Kensit character to fully justify this statement. Undoubtedly he is a clever man. His knowledge of books and book- men is surprising. He has read wisely and well ; and though we should hesitate to say that he was thoroughly grounded in English literature, yet his knowledge, if somewhat superficial, proclaims him to be one of the best read men residing in Run- corn. Of course, books have been to him a hobby from his boyhood days, and this accounts to some extent for his familiarity, with the British authors. He is a rabid teetotaler, and bears an inveterate hatred to tobacco; and as is usual with such per- sons is intensely self opinionated. The world, however, must be wrong so long as he is right. There is much in him which is highly commendable. But the virtues which he possesses are virtues which belong to the majority of persons. And it cannot be said that he bears any striking virtue which lifts him out of the common. After all it is the common-place man who is the Wier= on which the whole of society turns,and so for this reason the ordinary individual is by no means to be despised. He is,at all events,au industrious and painstaking person and bears an unimpeachable character. He has a dry sort of humour which he occasionally puts into practice much to the discomfort of those upon whom he practices. Were there wore persons who would display the same perseverance we have no hesita- tion in saying that there would be infinitely more successful men in the town of Runcorn.</p>
        <p>BPIGRA.M. A man whose I ife c.mveys the truth, That what is sown in days of youth, Fixes as though by lab,mred plan, The :Altura of the coming man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>TUBERCULOSIS.</head>
        <p>TUBERCULOSIS.</p>
        <p>fItPORTLNT EXPERINIENTS, BY THE CH.ESEURE contry" COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Tee Cheshire Ceunty Council recently undertook a series of esperinams witn herds at their dairy institute at Worleston and their agricultucel silllo3i at Holtriee Chapel, with a view not only of purging tnere from animals aff..cted with tuberculosis, but of demonstrating the reliability of the tuoarculin teat in diagnosing tuber- culesie, and ascertaining the am mat of lass that would be suffered in dolling with the disease, and whether or not a c Jre may be effected at a reasonable cost. 'rue work undertaken is to a very great extent educative on the farmers' be ialf, experiments being carried out fro a. thorough and drast c manner, and at an outlay such as toe farmer could not be expected to meet. The extreme impertance of this question to the farmers of Cheshire will be seen when it is remembered that the number of Cows and heifers in tee county of Chester in ctif or in milk in 1898 ve .s 105,198, giving an average ot 195 cows to every 1,000 acres of land in crops or in gems. No otaer couety comes anywhere near Caeshire in the average quantity of COW 3 pr 1000 acre?. Toe Caeshire County Counacil appointed a special committee to deal with this question, and on tnis committee every farmer in the county council was placed. Mr. Ltithwoed, the chief veterinary inspector for Caeshire,was employed to conduct the tests, and generally to give advice and assistance in carryieg out all experiments required. The hens were tested with tuberculin with tile following results :—ln the Worleston herd, numbering 54, 37 wore found to be healthy, 14 reacted to tee test, and hence were classed as Luber- culoue, and three were classified as doubtful. In the Holmes Cuaoel herd 17, 13 were found to be healthy, three tuberculous, and one doubtful. Of those clasaifiA as doubtful, one had just celvel, two were about to do so, and the other had a high temperature before injez- tiara, hence it WAS not conaidered 'de at a reliable conclu- sion could be come to in these oases under the circum- stances named. In each herd was a valuable shorthorn pedigree ball, both of watch reacted. ' Tne next step was to prove the reliability of the test by slaughtering those wnich bad reacted and having a careful and thorough post-mortem exammatioa of the bodies made by reliable men. It was, however, deemed advisable to retain a few of those WiliCO were coosiderel tuberculous for furteer experiments. Accordingly, ar- rangements were made for ten animals—including the two bulls—to be taken to the abetteir, Monchester, and there slaughtered, and the bodies examined by Mr. King, chief veterinary inspector her Manchester ; Mr. Storrar, chief veterinary inspector fer the city and counay of Chester; and Mr. Llithwooi, chief veterinary iusilector for Cueshire County Council. Arrangements were further made with Professor Delepine, of Owens Celleee, Manchester, that should any difficulty arise in detecting tuberculosis in any of the carcases he should he called in to make a microscopic and physiological examination so that no douot might remain. Again, in order to test whether or not the milk of the cows that had re- acted was tainted with tuberculosis, a sampain from ince cow giving milk was sent la a special sterilised bottle to Peofessor Delepine, to be analysed bacteriologically and physioloeically. Mr. Laithwood examine i the ud- ders of all the cows that had reacted at the dairy insti- tute. Worleston, and found only one with an indurated udder, the remainder; so far as could be ascertained by manual manipulation; being entirely free. The slaughter of the animals and tile poet-modem ex- amination took place a: Manchester ea tee Bth instant, in the presence of three members of time Cheshire County Council and the organising secretary. Of the eight cows sent, two were in lull milk, ha dog but recently calved, and were low in flesh; one had an indurated udder. One, a little over two months off calving, was a heavy milker, and a lean shelly cow ; one cow was fat and ready for sale, and another in very good con lition. Hence they represented all classes, and conseqa ently the Loss on them may be taken as approximating, if net ex- ceeding, the averaee of wow; wound ensue by sacrificing animals at Once under such comiitiene. Tee veterinary iaepectors found every animal affected with tuberculosis , more or less, and have since made a detailed report of each. The disease was chiefly found in the glaeds,lunes and liver, aril in the majority of eases the inspectors</p>
        <p>certified that the " carcases were otherwise very good." The examination is reg,arded as strong evidence of the reliability of the test. Toe exemination, of course, was most complete. being caraful:y done by experienced men, who not only knew what they were looking for, but exactly where t 3 look. All the carcases were declared suitable for human food, and they were placed in the hands of a sal-se-lan, who disposed of them at prices varying from £22 Is 81 to £4 141 ld (less expenses). Tho nine animals, including the bull (which fetched £22 is 81), reali,sed a total of £97 7s. All the cattle had been carefully valued before being sent away,such valuation beiag their worth Co Cue farmer as milking cattle or dairy stock. The total value of the nine animals was £149 184 61. Their sale pro- Iduced £97 7s, showing a total loss o £52 11s 61. The wile loss was in the cows which had recently calved. Being heavy milk!rs, they were tom in tl,sh. Hence, though high in value as dairy cows, they ware of low value for selling as meat. It is very apparel-it, however, that so great a loss neel not be incurred by the farmer, who weuld not be required to send his cattle away in this manner, but would select the best way and most suitable time for disposing of animals that reacted. The remaining cows that reacted to the test have been iso- lated from the rest of the herd, and furth.r experiments will be conducted to ascertain if tubnculosis can be curet The experiments already carried out show that the disease is not hereditary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>HILTON PUBLICA.N FINED OR PERMITrING DRUNKENNESS.</head>
        <p>A HILTON PUBLICA.N FINED OR PERMITrING DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At ths Rune'nu Petty Sessions, oa Mlnday, before Messrs. James Handley (in the chair), and F. J. Norman, a very lengthy case was heard in which Frederick Taylor, the landlord of the Red Lion lan, Halton, was charged at tne instance of the police with unlawfully permitting drunkenness on his premises, on Sunday tee 1202 of March.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Cooke, solicitor. Winsrord. a.potnred for the police, and Mr. Arthur Browne, Warrington, for the defonce. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Cooke stated the fact 3 of the came, which appeared ta be that a man named Feeney, an Irishmae, went to the Red Lion on the 12th of March. The first occasion would be from abou: half-past twelve to nearly half-past two, and he had a certain quantity of drink during that time. He again visited tue house in the evening from about six o'clock to ten, or a few minutes to tan during which time ha was more or less drinking. A few minutes afterwards he wa3 found by the police helpleisly drunk underneath the taproom window of the Inn. The man could not walk, talk, or do anything, and the upshot was that the officer who found him these had to get a cart in which to take him to the Police Station. Tuere could be no questien as to his being drunk. The only qiestion that might arise might he one of law ; and yet he failed to see even where a question of that kind could come in. He asked in the event of a conviction that the Bench in fairness to the man who con lucted his house properly, and as a warning to others, that the licsnie be endorsed. For if their Worships were lonient with the man, it would be no inducement to other public las te conduct their houses properly.—At this paint Mr. Browne asked whether it was a prosecution or a porsecution.—Mr. Cooke said he would silt:Alit to no interference.</p>
        <p>John Feeney, said he was an Irish Labourer, employed on the farm at Mr. Bites, Sutton. On the Sunday in question, he remembered going te the Red Lion, flatten, shortly after 12-30 and stopping there until it was close on 2-30, Duriug that time no had two or three pints of beer. Ile then returnel to his work on tee farm. He I visited the house again at about twenty minutes past six in tee evening, and that time he stayed until nearly ten o'clock. He remembered having two pints of beer, and also drinking out of a half-gallon jug. He himself paid for one half-gallon as well as the two pints. There were a number of people there, and he and his friend to the number of ten or t ?mitre were drieking out of the same half-galon as was their custom. Others paid for half-gallons as well as he. Tne drink was oupelied by the landlord and his wife. When he enteral the public house he had about 2s 8 1 with him, and on leaving ' thought he had about is 5d or is 61. Ha did not re- member what took place oatside the public-house, and the next thing he remembered was finding himself locked up, and the police informed him th at he was found helplessly drunk with only 21 upon him. He did not know how he gat to the police station, but if he had walked he would have remembered. He had !mown the landlord for about firceou years.—Cross-examined by Mr Browne, He' had no watch and could not s Amer to tee time. Be believed the bells had stopped and would not be surprised to hear it was nearly seven whea he went to the Inn. He had not been in the house for some week; previously. He could not tell exactly how many were present in the mem. On leaving the house he went out perfectly straight, and slid goen-night to Mr. Taylor. He had heard of a broken stick, and all the sides of his hips were blackened. Re left the house before ten o'clock, and he was well aware that Mr. Taylor insisted on closing the house at two minutes to ten. There was no row in the tap room which was a very small room, in deed there was hardly any room for them to move in. He could not tell why he did not go home with his com- panions. The beer he had in the afternooe (Id net effect him. He could not tell whether it was the fresh air that affected him but it might have been.—By Mr. Cooke: He had never been drunk on fresh air before (laughter), and they could take it it was the beer that got him int that state. Police-Const :tale Juane, stationed at Helton said on Sunday the 12,h of M troll whei near to the Red Lion Inn, flatten, he noticed some Irishmen coming from that direction. On going there ha found the last witness lying beneath the taproom window. He tried to rouse him ana whilst doing so Arthur Williams and another men came up. With their assistance Feeney was out into a cart and taken to Runcorn Police Station. The tine was about ten thirty. Feeuev could neither walk nor talk. Upon searching Feeney 2el was found upon him. Witness afterwards returned to Halton and sew the defendant; it would be about 11.45. In conversa- tion with him Taylor said he (sal served Feeney with beer in the early part of tbe afternoon and that the man left at about 10 o'clock appearing te be all right as far as he could see. Witness told him abet finding Feeney lying beneath the Tap-room window and that he had taken him to Runcorn and locked him up. Taylor re- plied " The devil you did ; he seemed all right and I should have supplied him with another pint if he had asked me." He had seen the landlord on a previous day in company with Sergeant Bourne. Last Thursday night the landlord had a conversation with him how he thought he was going on with the case.—Defendant in- terrupting said that Juette wag not apeaking the truth. —Continuing witness stated that defendant also wanted him to have a drink,bue be would not, By Mr.7Beewne Witness had t old them all that had taken place. There was a man named Johnson with him at the time. He did not take Johnson with him into the Red Lion for the purpose of getting him a drink, and he did not ask Mr. Caylor to give Johtison a drink beeause he had been assisting him. He did not say anything to Taylor aneut getting Feeney out of the country. But he heard the first of that story last night. He would swear that he del not say to Taylor, "I would g o and get hold of Feeney and get him to slip the country to quash the case."—Mr. Browne was press- log this point when the Ceairman objected saying the witness had already replied twice to tee questioa.—Mr. Browne contended he was about to warn tee witnees, as he had Mx. and Mrs. Taylor an! the man Johnsen to come forward and prove that he did say so, fieweeer, he bowed to the decision of the Bench.—Witness did break his stick upon the defendant ti rouse him. The stick was a "perished" one. Alfred Williams, farm labourer, corroborated the statements of the preceding witness, as to assistine in getting the man into the cart wraith he went to ft-tch. Tne man Johnson was the one that went with Jeette ti Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Bourne gave evidence of visiting the house on the sth March and ca,utiened the defeadane about giving so much beer te the Irishmen. e Mr. Browne for the defenee,c onteartd it was the duty . of the police not to persecute but to simply lay down the facts of the case. He thought Viet the case had been stated rather unfairly. Bet to considn- the facti of the CA9O, they were not in dispute. It was perfectly true that Feeney WAS in the house, hut 'whilst there he was perfectly sober. There wasenothine wrone in the men being in the pablic house. It was a common cus- tom and one in vogue all over the place tit these Irish- men assembled in some recognised public-heu ie. Where else hal they to go ? And in these eeatherinee thee talked over all things connected with their besinese aod also with their client:v. The Mtn may have stave.' in the public-house 6 hours provided he was net drank, disorderly, and that he was sober and the faudlord allowed him to. There was always a division between drunkenness and soterrrss. For instance a man might be perfectly sober in the house and the moment he went out into the air he became affected. That M. 13 a mater of common knowledge and a fact well-known by these persons who frequented p iblic-houses. And be would submit that such was the Case in the present case. lin had one complaint to melee and that was that the twin Johnson was not present. He was a most mete.riai wet- ness, and lie bad been given to understand that be was to have appeared for the police. He contended that it was the duty of the police to see that Geerge Johesou was there as he would have deubeless, tdd the truth of the affair. Frederick Taylor, the defendant, said he was the licensee of the Red Lion Inn,llalton. It was a fact that Irishman did Come to his house, and it was also true that he stopped until a few rniuutes to ten ; but the j whole lot of them were turned out a fear minutes to ten, as he made a point of closieg his house at two minutes to ten. He remeenberel serving mix half gal- lons to 16 men which constituted Feeney's party, and there were three or four in the other party. There was no noise or row, and he was ennatAntiv I, and out of the rae•rooni. It was a very small heuee and he thought that the whole house) c.:mtif be. put into the court room. There WAS also a res.rty oc fur Enelish-s' men drinking with Faeuey'e party. Oen of the men Dodd was present in the court. He renarmia2red Feeney vie, out of the house. lie go!: up from his seat near the deer, and he recollected he was the secend man go- ing out. Flo said " Geed night Mr. Taylor," and witnese replied" Good night John." Tura was no sign of his</p>
        <p>being drunk. Later in the night Constable Juette and George Johnson came to the house. Juet•,e said to hint " You might give Johnson a drink." Witness expressed astonishment, and Juette added "It is all right, he has been assisting me.' Then Juette told him he had takes Feeney to Runcorn and that he found him underneath' the taproom window. Juette also threatened in a small way that he was going to summon him for permitting drunkenness. That was how he understood it. Juette then got him out by himself in the shop and said "For Christ's sake don't let this come out or I will net the b-- sack in a week." He then told him to tell-Feeney to slip the country. Witness gave Johnson a drink as he was not aware whether he was doing right or wrong. He would swear that Feeney was perfectly sober when he went out.—Cross.examined by Mr. Cooke: There was nothing about Feeney's behaviour to indicate that he was anything like half drunk let alone as drunk as he had been described. As far as he could calculate, there were abeut 25 men drinking on the property that night. He noticed all the men and there was not one of than the worse for drink when they left the house. He last saw the man George Johnson either on the Saturday or Thursday in company with Constable Juette. Witness did not speak to him, but he (Johnson) spoke to witnees about the case. He did not ask him to cerne as a wit. ness ;he volunteered. He was no friend of Johnsen's, and Johnson had only usad the house the same as many others. Questioned as to whether he had seen and spoken to two Irishmen who were to have appeared an witnesses, Taylor said he had spoken to the men prior to the court, and wanted them ta come to the Wilson Hotel.—Mr. Cooke: Ah, to give them more drink Mr. Browne : I was waiting for them there.—Mr Cooke: You need not have said that. —Witness continuing said that Feeney had also spoken to him about the affairs and said he did not remember anything after leaving the house. Hs would swear that the Constable asked them to serve Johnson with drink, and stated Johnson had "assisted " him. He under-stoed Juette to be hinting that he was going to bring a charge against him. Mrs. Emma Taylor,wife of the last witness, eorrobera. ted her husband's statement in the main. Feeney on going out said" Good night." He walked all right. and did not need any assistance. She drew the beer with ' which she served Johnson in the presence of Constabls Juette, and Juette also had a glass of "peppermint." Cress-examined by Mr. Cooks: Feeney earns to the house by himself about an hour before the bulk of the other Irishmen. Asked as to whether she thought an Irishman who had an hour's drinking start of his friends would be more likely to be affected. she reaßel that she could not say. She did not think Feeney was any more drunk than the others, and they were nothing out of the way for Irishmen. (Laughter.) She was pre?ent when Sergeant Bourne earns to the house before, and know hex husnand had been cautioned.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Sarah Ann Mellor, wife of William Henry Mellor, stald she went to th 3 Inn on the Sunday night for her supper be3r at about half-past nine. She occasionally assisted in the house. There was no noise or disturb- ance.—By Mr. Cooke : She did not think the Irishiney were playing chess ; neither c)uld she say what they were doing. Asked if she had over known a number oE Irishmen to be quiet after two houri drinking, witaeas made no reply.</p>
        <p>The eviden-ce being concluded, the Bench retired, and on returning into court tha Chairman said they had given the case their bast consiJaration and had decided to convict. Taylor for oerinitting drunkenness, would be fined 203 and costs 424 61 (including the usual advo- ctte's fee). The license would not be endorsed this time.</p>
        <p>John Feeney, farm 1 abourer, of ;Sutton was then charged with bin g drunk and disorderly in the high- way, and also with being drunk on licensed prernise3, on the 12th of Nfarch.—Ele pleaded guitry.—la considera. tion of the valuable aosistance he had given till police the case was not pranced, and Feeley was disenarged en payment of a fine of 5;. Toe Bunch ho2sd it wo aid be a warning to him.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The Urban District', Council Election will take place at Runcorn on Siturday (to-morrow). There will be tvo contests, Bridgewater Ward having to choose between Mr.W.Shaw, junionand Mr. W. Handley;And Newtown Ward having two candidates in till persons of Dr. Mclimzif., and Mr. Samuel Walton: The polling booths will be at the National Schools and the Board Scnools respectively and will b 3 open from 12 noon to 8 p.m. As the polling day draws nearer more interest is being manifested in the fight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INQUIRY</head>
        <p>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INQUIRY</p>
        <p>AT F RODS LIAM. £3,400 WANFED FOR WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>As the result of a petition from the Frodshain Lord- ship Parochial Committee to the Local Government Board askinz permission to borrow £3,400 fer the par- po=es of water supply, Mr. E. Sandford Fawcett, A.M.1.C.E.. one ol the Local Government Board's In- spectors, held an inquiry at the Boys' School, Overton, on Wednesday morning. There was a number of in- idesntial ratepayers present, in addition to Mr. George Ashton (deputy clerk to the Runcorn. Rural District Council, and clerk to the Parochial C7rnmittee), Mr W Timmins (engineer ti the scheme), Mr Henry They (chairman of the Parochial Committee and Parish Cour- cil), Mr W Diggle (surveyor to the Rural Distirct Coun- cil), Mr Alfred Thomas, J.P., Mr John Oekleston, Mr Charles R.ynolds.J.P., Mr C E lainaker (agent to the Marquis of Cholmendeloy), Rev H BB! ogg (vicar), Mr S Davies (architect). Mr D Timmins, Ur Edward Rhodes, etc., etc. .N a one came forward to oppose the application. From the evidence adduced it would appear that the area of the Lordship was 2639 acres; p)puistinn (last census) 1,513, (escimatod pr-seat time) 1,580. The assessable value was £1,365. Oatstancling loans amounted to £1,985 (including a RIM of £3OO sanctioned but not yet harrowed). • There were no existing water mains in the Lsrdship, and no arrangements, except the public and private walls and pumps, for the supply of water to the district. By the proposed scheme it was intended to construct a new reservoir upon Beacon Hill, and to pump water from the works of the Frodshant. Township to it, and to supply the parish by gravitation. A lengthy resolution, which was stated to have been agreed to by both the Township and taa Lordship wE then submitted.</p>
        <p>The Inspectar having dealt with the details of struc- ture and other pertinent paints, examined the plans submitted by Mr. W. Timmins (the ea2inner). Mr. Timmins also submitted the vsnits at 49 s.parate analysea of water in the diitrict taken at various periods from 1879. It was intended that nothing less than 3 inch mains should be used. The supply per head at the last inquiry was carefully worked out to bs 12 gallons per _head:</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Rhodes, a ratepayer. stat.d it seemed to be a very urgent case. Personiily, he knew that the water supply he had to use wal not a safe one, and everything nad to be boiled. He also thought that it was yeti, requisice that they hmild hay..., a water supply not only for domestic purposes, but as a means of flush- ing tie drains. He attributed the pr ,valene,a of diphtheria and typhoid in till district lately ta the want of an adecinats supply of water, and the sooner they had such a water supply the better. The Inspactor : Was this very prevalent ? Mr. Rho lea : There wore several cases in the district laFt year and a year or to ago. The Insp3ctar : You have opan miu-hole covers on tin seweis and sirens arise ?</p>
        <p>Mr. iihnies : I b3lieve O. Anyway, there, is no means of flashing thr3m.</p>
        <p>They atoke-I wn—e-thsr the reference to the prevaw lenco of diptheria, and typhoid applied to the township or Lordship,</p>
        <p>Mr. Itho-Jes T. the district ; I can only testify ptrsonally to the diphtheria, in the Lordship. Mr. J. Oclzleston wished to concur with all that had been 'aid. He bilievel that M. Tioanfns had con- ducted experiments and tai suns down about 183 feet in F.earch of water but cluld not find a good supply. He was also aw.tre of the pfevalsocs of illness. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Linatterr (re.ores-en-lini—t-I).e. Marquis of Chohnlndeley, and ether iufluential Isneowlers) gtated he could tendon° all. that 113.1 been Fad of the rivd of the wster supply for Frodsham Lordship. He kinu'd, howewr, Uk bsuzipat that the reservoir be erected itz such a positiou that would command the greatist pare of the Lordship, so that if anyone built housn they could he supplied. _ _</p>
        <p>The ',ler:if:tor : Mut you think th, reservoir is elevate I enough? _ .</p>
        <p>Mr.Linalzer-said h I was of °plain that higlier ground coald b ob!;airied for not;hinz. The sitt3. had in view would not move the reservoir nor a thm 60 or 100 yards from the propo3pd one, whilst it would enabie it to command a diitrict which the proposed res3rvoir would not: command.</p>
        <p>The Inqpector questioneJ Mr. Li:la:hr os t) wither he thon,lht there would b many houseq boil ? Mr. Liniker furthor statPd that the scheme was a very. 5,,U01 one and one that had been want for a long time, he was only afraid that the houqes at Bank, Nickledale .2.nd Bnel:the:Ws honses viniA not bs suppliet Mr. A. Thotnaq was of opinion that the scheme would enable water to 13 put into "Mersey View" and he did not think there was another house in the Lordship any lilzher.</p>
        <p>N) further evidence beinz offered the Inspcetor closed the inquirg, and visited the sites in queition.</p>
        <p>MR. GEORGE BARTON'S FARE WELL CONCERT. On Saturday evening, Mr. George Barton gave 4 farewell cancert in Pate Hall, in connection witlt the sries he is giving in those towns he has visited during his vocal career. There was rather a poor attendanc,). The Bohemian Glee Singers gave some excellent items, and Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins was suc- cessful with "Good-bye, Sweetheart, good-bye." Mr George Birton's efforts were well received. and Mr, Frank Walker gave some humorous sketches in spirited manner. Mr. James Beale was applauded for his contributions, and had a recall. Mrs. W. B. Roberts interpreted two pianoforte solos most cred-. itably. Songs by Mr. A. Marshall received capital treatment. Miss Emilie Taite received an encore • for her rendering of" Dear heart." Mr Percy RIM dall accompanied in an efficient manner.</p>
