        <p>WIRRINGTON BOROU.;Ei POLIOE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Befors the Mlyor (chairman). Alderman Roberts, Councillor Smethurst. and Mr. James lienherd. A Dim)Ero PANIILy —williarn Phillips, 2), Powys-street, Was charged with assaulting his mother (Louisa Phillips) on the 14th vtarch.—Defen lant pleaded lilt; guilty.—Complain- ant sad after 80719 ta'king abmit insurance pelicel her ron knocked her down. blackened her eve and kicked her. Ada Phillips, sister to defend trtt, said her mother struck defend- ant and afterwards banv,ed her own he'd on the fisor and said she would make him responsible for it, and she also bit him. —Mrs. Fox said complainant was drunk on the &amp;1.7 In question. Ph defonliant never struck his mother ; th 3 latter fell down herself on the flags and knocked her eyes. The Beech dismissed t'ae case.</p>
        <p>A. Flom, NEAR TVFIGGERY FARM. —Da.lf 91 Cope'. Rol,son- street. was charzed with assail ting Michael Flannigan, Woolson. on the 13th Harch.—Oosinlainalt slid that on the morning in question he met defend mt near the Pwig'zery Farm who asked him what he hal been saving at) ml him (defendant). Witness turned round when defendant struck him knocking him on the floor and as, he was 2ettinz up defendant struck him eigain.—Derend ant 9 dri th fiztat but defendant got the best of it.- The case was dismissed. SA TURDLY. —Before Mr. R. T. Fair:3'oll;h (thair:um). and Mr. Geddes.</p>
        <p>A. LAZY FATHER.-403.0 Slnvi, 21, Yo-k -street, wa3 charged with neeeetinz his three children, filed ten. eight and B ',hearer, who prolecuted, said it was the third time p-isoner ha I been brou ;ht. no on th tt ch trge. The man hal got into bed company and had repea`e,ily de• setter' the children.— %Ir. F,nk Aukland,relieving off: er for the digtrict, state i he had visited the case dozens of limes, The children had been taken to th I workhouse. and direetty prisoner got to know the 7 were there he took them ont. and after working a we ‘k left the district. —Oars Shaw, mother of the prisoner, said her son lived w:th her It wis drink that kept him from work, and that kind of thing had been going on for about six months —sergeant Cheney statel that, 'prisoner made the admission to him tint hewill. not work because his wages were not suffldent for him, as he only earned 54 per hour.—The bench sent him to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>NONDLY.-1343re Mr T. Blrehtll (In the chair). Cluncitiors Smethurst and Shaw Green, and Mr. S. Wright. TEMP MtRY Auraortrer —Mr. Jenkins made an apPlica- tinn re the estate of George Bloomfield, who he explained, died a short time since, his widow dying last week. The ap- Vication was that her son Robert who. W 49 0119 of the executors under the will, shlttld hold the off-license for tne present premises at 83 Longford-street. The nroperty was to be offered for safe.— ramporary authority to sell was granted until transfer day. AN lICCFSTRIC 4oßw..—Two youths. William Burns, hawker. Mill-street, and Joseph Longshaw. wker,Oarnbria- place, were brought up chlrgei with frtrio isly driving a bore.—P.C. Smith deoo,ed that at five o'clock on Saturday, the llth March, he WAS crossing the footbridge at Arefev 'When he saw each of the defendants fariougly driving a horae attached to a cart. 'MeV were Vital over Warrington Bridge and each WAS thrashng his horlo with the whipstlek. Thy rushed along the Arpley-road and turned into B ild- street Witness followed, and when near the crossing saw that one of the ant-nets had fallen. Councillor Tinnioa, the Town Clerk and others cemplained to hi es of the conduct of the defendants. which Was disgraceful.— f'he Chairman : Were they racing ? --P.C. Smith replied that they were.— Longahaw : We were only going at the rate of seven rnil rs an hour. —Burns r I could not he`p it. Wh-n my horse gr”e. up a" brat)" it jurnns into the air. (Laughter.) —The Chairman : It is evidently a vary valuable ant nal —a flying horse (Renewed laughter )—A. tine of 51 and costs. 75,. was Imposed in each case.</p>
        <p>.117F.SDAY.—Before Blrchall (eh drman), Coencillor Shaw Green, and Messrs Garnett and Geddes, CRUELTY TO 4. HOR9K.—Nw.c3 BtaOrl was char.rd with being drunk and disorderly whilst in citarge of two horses and a trap in Xtertey-stre.t, the )revieus night and with ill- triatting a horse by crave; ig it- whilst in an unfit state.— Defe idant pleaded not guilty to the seeonti chirge —Sers'eant Smith said he was in company with Sergeint Bell when de- fendant and another min with a trip tome 4 into blersey- street, One of the horses was attached to the trap behind Defendant was drunk and they took them to the pollee station. Whilst there they found the horse :it tti.. rear of the nhandry had a severe out on the near should 3r two inches long and six inehss deep anti it was trernedug all the time.-- Sergeant Bell corroborated and stated defendant ad nitted to in he had driven the hor e 14 miles with the wound on its side. —'4lr. Hewitt, veterinary surgeon, also corroborated.— Defendant w u.s fined 5s and costs for the first offence &lt;IRA ordered to pay the costs amounting to 15s for the second offence. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A LERSON To RECIEXIBER.—DanieI Boyle, a repeatable looking man, was charged with being drunk and disorcleny on Winwick rd., on Sloniav night and with assaulting Eliza- beth Knock.—P.C.Hurst said he was going towards the yorse and Jockey when he heard a woman shouting. Witness found ,defeadant. drunk. He had knocked a jug out of the girl's band. Defendant said it would take six policemen to talk him to the poiice eation.—Elizabeth Knock said the defendant followed her down Winwick-road_ knocked the .jug out of her hand and paihed her on the floor.—The Chairman said it was lucky far defendant the poliecrn in was on the scene or his position might have been much more serious. He was fined 51 and acsts in each care or seven clays. " SCIENTIFIC KNOWLEDGE Goes ar re Pacxu: c." --An elderly man named Edward Sherlock. applied for a vaccina- tion exemption certificate for one of his children, who he -stated. was corn on th, 28th December I tst.—The Chairman : / hops you have thought ever the matter. --Applicant: I have. sir —Councillor Shaw Green : What are the circum- stances ? --The applicant inadt it, reply unt,l the Chairman asked him if the child tvel h-althy, when ha replied •• Yes - sir ; I think the best means of keeping it so is to keep it fr int vaccination. I have a strong obje.ttiou to vaccina- ti. ~f.--The Chairman: You differ f,oni a great number of people who are supposed to possess scientific knowledge about these matters.—Applicant : Yes but a great deal of that sci ntific knowledve goes by the pocket. Laughter) Further questioned, Mr Sherlock said he bad had other</p>
        <p>CARTER'S LITTLE LIVER PILLS.</p>
        <p>Cure Torpid Liver, Bile.. Sallow Complexion, and Sick Irrftdach3 promptly : awl. rlie.Ai so as to =ed. ChenAst3,ls.</p>
        <p>children who had suffered from the effects of vaccination in the breaking out of ulcers over the body. He always had a strong objection to vaccination. The child was born in Croft.—The Cler.i : Where da you live now —At first the applicant, said he lived at Croft, and afterwards explained that it was at Newchureh, whereupon Mr White told hint that the ma.tistratds had no jurisdiction. and that he would have to apply to the maglstrates Bitting at the County Petty cessions. He then left the court evidently surprised. WEDNE3DIY.—Before Mr. Geddes (chairman) and Alder.</p>
