        <p>OCCASI3NAL £lCEN3E.—Coillieillor Shlllcank. of Vie By Horse, Winwiek-street. applied for an oe sisional •n se sell at the Trades and Industrial Exhibition on the Ist, 3rd, 4th and 6th April —Granted. .</p>
        <p>_ TEMPORARY AIITZIORITY.—Temporary anthorltv we granted to W. W. Whittle to sell tt the" Ring O'Rells," Ohurch-street, in place of William Mairoy, and James Thomas 133te to sell at the "Royal Oak" beer house in SL. James' street in lieu of Robert Eyes. BITING A CONSTABLE.—Margaret Hood was charrd von being drunk and disorderly in the fair ground the pr-vious night and with ass ,ulting P.O. Healey. P.,J. Healey salt the defendant became very violent when he took her to the police station. She bit him about the legs and arm 3 and he had to have assistance.—Rood was tined 5s and costs.</p>
        <p>_ A DEIR FIT OF TEMPER.-JOhlll Loneshaw. a young MM. was charged with committing wilful damage to the amount of 30s at the Central Hotel, Horsemarket street.—Mr. James Wooden, licensee of the Central Hotel add the defendant came in about 10-30 on Tuesday night and smashed an embossed glass value 30s.—Defendant said he did it in a fit of temper ' • he had had a dispute with someone before coin- ing in the house. —The Bench fined him 103 and costs and ordered him to pay the damage. TIIIIII3DAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman; and</p>
