      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Alderman Sutton.</head>
        <p>Alderman Sutton.</p>
        <p>A NEGLECTFUL HUSBAND —Thomas Gilligan. Teft's-yard. Hall-street, was charged with running away from the Borough. leaving his wife and three children chargeable to the common funds of the Warrington Shearer, relieving officer, informed the lii.non that prisoner had keen arrested on a warrant issued in 1896. He was now in regular work and IA A maintained his children since. With the per- mission of the magistrates he would now withdraw the case. —The Bench agreed to his course being taken. HER 60TII APPEARAvCE.-1111en Connor made her sixtieth appearance before the Bench. ttis time-charged with being drunk and disorderly in Glasshouse-yard at 11-30 ths previ- ous night.—P.C's Carter and Plumb proved the case, and the magistrates committed her to the prison for one month. hard labour. _ _</p>
        <p>Too LATE I—Two applications wore m tale for exemption orders under the Vaclination Act, but the Deputy Magis- trates Clerk (Mr. 4. F. F. White) ascertained that in one case the child was five months old, and in the second, that it was 41 months old. lie pointed out that exemption orders could only be issued within four months of the birth of the child.—The Chairman said certificates could not be granted, and the children would h aye to bi vaccinated as the appli a- tions or exemption had not been made within the stipulated time.</p>
        <p>--- SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASFS.—The following eases were dealt with for irregular atten lance at school at the inst ance of Mr. Jones,school visitor: Richard Haddolk.3.Welsh's-yard, 2s 6d each in two eases; Henry Brookes.. 21. Dutton-st? eet, 28 6d; Rob?rt Cunliffe, 74. Catherine-street. order,d to scud the child to Wycliffe school ; John Gough, 14, Baxter-street, child ord,red to atteed Heathside school; Richard Newton 8. Robinson's-yard Fennell-street, ordered to attend school ; George Wilcock. 10, Baxtenstreet. ordered to Hes.lhside. school ; Allen Wilkinson, 41 Hoyle-street, case adjourmd or a month.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETrY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MoNDAT.—Befere Messrs._James Handley (presiding)</p>
        <p>ami F. J. Norman.</p>
        <p>LICENSING.—Henry De Korte, licensee of the Holyheal Harbour, was granted temporary authority to sell at the George Inn, and also to retain his license of the Holyhead Harbonr.—Mr. El. N. Linaker, who made the application ex- plained that the circumstances arose through the person who was to have taken the Holyhead Harbour by transfer having withdrawn.—The police made no oajection. DRUNK ON LICENSED PRE3llSES.—Hortense Mclawan, a married woman, was summoned for being drunk on licensei premises to wit the Wheatsheaf Hotel, Bridge-street on the Saturday night previous, and with refusing to quit at the re- quest of the landlord..—Henry Farmer (the well-known Runcorn forward) stated that about 7-39 prisoner Came to his house in company with seine other worra n and they were served with three beers and a porter. He then went into one of the other rooms, bet hearing a noise he went to see what was the matter with the women. He found prisoner in an excited stets and asked her to be quiet. He then heard the other weineu accuse her of stealing something. .but he aid not believe that. He then three times in succession asked her to go out of the house. She refused Prisoner's husband also asked hsr to gs out, and tried to persuade her. He then pinned the woman's arms and his wife took a cup off her. In the struggle the weman bit him severely. and he had to go to Mr. Speakman's, the chemist, to have the wound attended to, in answer to the Bench, prisoner said she must have been drunk, and asked to he excused. She also made a voluble statement in which she explained that she had been drinking with some other people in a public house until they coula not get anyone to serve them. A little woman of the company then said she would take them to a house where they would get served. Whilst in the " Wheatsheaf " she was aceusea of not being her hiss- band'a real wit/a. The accusation, which was not true, made her excited and she could not rtaile-stani what she was doing. .:She had come from Liverpool with her husband who was a fistmen.-1 he Chairman said she placed herself in a v ry serious position, and might also hive placed the land- lord into a serious position ; especially if he had served her with env more drink. Under the ciicumstences, she would be-let off with a flee or 54 an 1 sslcosts, or arreen de—s. Boa BURGIARS —Victor Abel (1l), Peter Bradbury (13) and Robert Bradbury (9), were breneht no charged with stealing a meloaeon valise 93, the property of alaineel Forster.—Tae boys pleaded guilty.—Samuel Perster said he was a boatman in the employ of Mr. Hersefield. and hal charge of the narrow beet "John." He lived at 15, Tavior's-..ova, Halt ln-ro . On aatuedsy week. the 11th, he left his flit all safely locked up in the aril basin. neer the Customs atonee On the twelfth he found his melorleon MiftSiTlT. and adentifiea the o e prradiree i as his proeerty. —air. Georg s aforsefield.laaltort - lane, said he was the owner of the flit. Ho had examined it and fou .d a part of the cabin doer hr iken off. He knew no`hine about the theft —Police C mstahle Harry Fry Rtltel he appeehen led the boys as the result of inquiries. On healg chareel they admitted the off ince. The bigeest boy st ste he broke in the door, ant the others want in and teak ail meloaeln out. —The fettle: of the Bridburys said the bays were not bad lads, but not perhaps whet they might to be. He lurther stated the meloaleon hid been breneht to his hens' and he had straightway gone to Forster about it. —Mrs Abel said she knew nothing else st it. and her bw had been very good the lest few weeas ; though previous to theta he hii caused her acme troehle throu th not coin,/ to school. --The hors promised the Bench thst thee werea 'serer again do such a thing, and after being severely csistionea they w re discharged The parents were besot over in 49s (each (sass+) to bring theirs vo for judgment within the next three months when called upon.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN POLICE COURT</p>
