      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>W ARRINGTO N7-FIRLD CLUB.</head>
        <p>W ARRINGTO N7-FIRLD CLUB.</p>
        <p>At the annual and entamological meeting,held in the Museum becture Room on Friday evening last week, there was a good attendance. The first part of the evening was epent in examining the beauti- ful displays of butterflies, moths, and. other in4eco, exhibited by various members. Messrs. Womersley and W. Mounfield subsea uently read notes on the last season's captures. and also enumerated some of the entomological observations they had, either alone or jointly made. alr. William Mounfield also showed and described a modification of a C )ntinen- tal method of moun'ing for exhibition single insects on wood under glass, and a case for showing series of specimens, with glass bottom as well as top,thus facilitating inspection. Messrs. L. Greening, Jack- son, Boiton, and Dr. White took part in the dis- cussion which followed,and Messrs. Womenley and Mounfield, having been cordially congratulated on their work, suitably replied. Mr. Councillor J. Shaw Green was unanimously re-elected president, and Mr. A. J Jolley, secretary. The number of mem- bers is increasing, and the vitality ot the club was manifest in an animated discussion which concluded the meeting. Provisional arrangements for several summer rambles were agreed upon; and anyone in- terested in natural history, and desirous of joining this .energetic and democratic society, may obtain full Information about its organization and methods either from the staff ot the Museum, whose collec- tions it is one of the dub' prime objects to enrich, r from the Preeident or Secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>One of the great organs of the scholastic profession contained a notification that dyspeptics must not apply for ,a certain appointment, the reason given being that they could not possess the equability of temper which was indispensable.</p>
