        <p>LANCASFIIRE AT,LIINCE. EAALESTOWN v. HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between these old local rivals was played on the Mesnes ground on Saturday. The first match which was pls.vei at Haydock a few weeks ago resulted in a pointless drew, but since then, Haydock have considerably r.--arranged their team and their team on Saturday contained four new men, all of whom were formerly connected with Lancashire League Clubs, viz., two with Nelson and two with (Thorley. Eerlestowa played toe identical eleven ti that which performed so well at St. Helees against Allingtoe the week before. Haydock woe the,tess and they set the home team to feels the hill and a strong wind. Hampaen set the ball in motion soon after half-pest three, in the preaence of quite 2,000 spectatsrs, a goolly number of these having acc empenird Haydeck. The first item of interest came from the home left who dribbled nicely and Mayor sent in a beauty frrn the extreme right, Chew just getting the bell away as the visitors forwards rushed for him' Grand tackieg on the part of Green and Parr again gave the home ferwards peresaion and Green passing to Flampaon this player scared Eiries- tewn's first goal with a splendid oblique shot. No one was more surprised than the .scorer and the Haydock custociian,aa the ball appeared to be travelling round the pest, when the wind seemed to catch it, and just drew it under the bar and into the'net. The point vvaa,however, received with great cheers by the Eirlestown portion of the crowd. Reused by this reveese the visitors forwards rushed down splendidly and d Aging the backs Heighten brought Chew to his knees with a daisy cutter. Again the Hayelack left got down aud Haves missed big kick and let in Deighton and Caew had to le ave his gra: in order to save. With the wind in their favour, Flaydsck still kept the home def .12e3 busy. No mere s.orinse was done up to toe interval when the score was :—Eariestown 1, Havelock nil. On crossing over Earlestown with the wind behin I them, soon le: the Heyde* defence know. that their position was going to be sinecure as Leach and dettlo dribbled finely dome the I.ft, the latter eventually causing I.em cansiderahle aneie'y with a shot which he jest tippl over the bar. flainpson cleverly tricked the Haydock backs and with- out steadying himself he baleed the bell in the net at a terrific rate. After this it was a ease of Earlestawa first, the rest n rwhore, and Haydock resorted to variou. tactics for wasting time. Just .before the finish, Leachi and Settle carried the ball kite ne Heyde* goal-mouth and the former shut hari at Lowe., The custodian caught the ball, but in at`,emptirsg to throw it away he fell, and before he could recover possession Leach pounced upon it, and scored the third goal, thus making the game secure. Final : —Earlestewr.3, Haydock nil.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTJN LAD DitOWNED IN' A ,‘Y PIT.</p>
        <p>In the Parade Room of th Police Station,WarriOten, on Monday afternoon, Mr. Jones, deputy coroner, con- dueted an inciniry resp,cting the death of a lad named Jam,s Au 4ustiae Finn,who was drowned in a clay pit at Flo raley on the previous Friday afternoon. Mr. F. R. Roberts, solicitor, represented the Firth Mattress Mei- panv, the ownera o the land wh-re the pit. is situated. The lint witness callecl was Sails Ann Finn, mother of the docwori, and wife of Patrick Finn, tanner's la- bourer, 3. Dikitr4-vard, who F.ail her son was six years of ay. 01 Friday afternoon, atntr, half•past three o'clock, the dee lasel left tha honse, and never came back alive. Sre did not know tin dista,nca of the pit as she had on,y ben living in the distfict ablut two weeks.</p>
        <p>Stroh Jane 13tnoroft a,p,5,1 tint a', 425 she was passing near to the pit on Frilly afterloon C 01914 she saw the d-ceasPi in the pit. Sne gave the Mar n and her brother weat t the assist inle ot tha l Ad. The nit was not fenced. Sne ha I lived in tne dithict about 25 years. The Coroner : Flow far is tin pit from the nearest hi.h way ? Witness: I should think it was about fifty yards distant.</p>
        <p>The Curnner : If the nit i within fifty yards it is blund to be fenced. It then comes within the quarry fening a-t. a slotion nt whin Op 11,1,r;04 ttrit If it is ogihin 50 ytrl front) the hihty it ghall he kept fenc,l off, and unless s ) kept it shall be d3enaed a nuis- .ano., undar the act. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Roberts proceeded to cro3s•examile VIP witnesg, ani elicited the fact that the clay pt cvAs wo:lred by Mr Welshy until ablul Christrnaq tim ‘. Since thsn it had not'been worked, and the water had accumulated.</p>
        <p>The Clroner said it wAs no; m4teria.l whether there was water in the pit. The only notnt wal whether it was withie 50 yar s from the public highway or place of public resort, and if 89 ir shwld:he fenced roun 1. Detective Barlow slid he had not rrilashrA the die- ta.nca, hut he should think ic was within 30 yards from the wareAt public highway. . . .</p>
        <p>John Thomas Bailey Bahl he wts cane I by his sister to the pit and took the deeemed on of the water. He was then dead.—By Mr. Roberts: 14 not the Firth Com- pany's mill i front of this pit P—Witneis : Yes.—Then it most ba mare than 50 yards from the highway ?—I cannot say. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Rnbsrt4 said the city pit was short'y to be worked again. The Firth Company htd only been in possession of thfe lao:1 ab ut six we4ks. H had telephoned for one of the members of the C)mpany, who would be able to proge the di.taece of the plc, troyn the highway. Hs had not yet arrived and he would ask for an adjnarnment. The Clrnner ajl if thl jury thought anything could he gaine.l by ,n adj-mrnment he would grant it. But if they Irnew the pl ice he dii not think therl W4S any necessity for it. If the Jury were of opinion thtt it was a dangerous pit, they could make a presentment to that eff•ct.</p>
        <p>Tne jury reurnel a verli3t of " tionidental death,' and added a nregentment to the effe:tt that the pit in its present condition was dangerous t) the public.</p>
        <p>FARM FIRE AT MOORE.</p>
        <p>A fire was discovered on Mrs. Bradley's fat m, at Moore on Saturday afternoon, The Wilderspool Fire Brizade was summoned, and Superintendent Thee, with Deputy-Superintendent Broughton and a fair complement of men, turned out with the steam engine Empress. They found three stacks of corn, clover, and hay in full blaze. It was thought impossible to sate the stacks, owing to the strong wind, and special attention was paid to the adjoin ing buildings, which contained a large quantity of oats, also a Dutch barn. The brigade were ably as- sisted by a number of men in turning the stacks over. The three stacks were destroyed,but the out- buildings and the barn were saved. The damage which is estimate at £2OO. i clvered bvinsurancs</p>
