      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>NEW DEPARTURE.</head>
        <p>A NEW DEPARTURE.</p>
        <p>Ttve first busine3s before toe in:eting was of an impor- tant ciaracter, namely, to consider t ie following notice of m .t.ton :—" Th-tt for a period of twelve months from the 20th M irch, 1899, the committ3e be empowered to remit the tranee fee of 103 6cl for all new membera wishing to join the club." greet deal of discussion ensued, and eventually a division was taken, when the President declared the motion carried by a small majority. It was pointed out by the supporters of the motion that the committ3a would still have power to re- fuse to admit any un Mairable porion ; and also that all that was intended wae to offor an inducement to the public to become) memly!rs of the club this year.</p>
        <p>THR ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>Tho accounti of the Hon Creasuror (Mr. W. H. Rob- inson) were submit'ei and passed, showing a debit bal- ance of £67 10s., as against a debit balance tlas previous year of £ll6 18.</p>
        <p>OPEN AIR FETE.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN Subautted a regolutinn to the eff •ct that the meeting approved of the open air fli!‘e to be hell in June next for the purpose of raising a sinking fund for the purchase of toe remaining shares in the Cricket Ground Cempany, Limited, by the Cricket Club.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOIIII seconded, and the motion wa carried, Messrs. Jtht Dewhurst, T Riftles, Dr Smith, C Leigh, and R r Fairclough being appointed trustees of the sinking fund.</p>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT AND OTHER OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. R. T. FAIRCLIIIGH seconded by Mr. LEIGH, it was %mania-lousily and 11-artily rsolved that Mr. William Pierpoint. who had so ably discharged the duties during the-previous year. be re-elected prfsi- that. Other officers wen appointed as follows :—Hon treasurer, Mr W H Robinson ; bon secretary, Mr. C H Uns worth ; match secr.lary, Mr Gordora Robeits ; cap- tain of the first team, Dr Smith ; deputy captain, Mr J W Bibby; captain of the second team Mr Ernest Woods; captain of the bowling club, Mr William Rie'sardson ; secretary, Mr. George Pilling ; tennis secretary, Mr. J C Robinson; auditor, Mr D Tiianion. The following gentlernee were eleated the committee for the ensuing year :—Cricket, Dr Smith. and Messrs J Callon, George Clarke, J W Bibby; bowling. Messrs R Siloock, J Dunn, William Richardson, and C Llgh ; tennis, Mr J Sut- cliffe. Nine new members were elected, and votes of thanks having been accorded to the President and other officers, and to Mrs. Kiernan for the use of the rootn,the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>SANGER'S CiRCIJS.</head>
        <p>SANGER'S CiRCIJS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday next Warrington will be favourei with a visit from Lord George (the original) Sanger's Circus. The whole of this eitahlishment has now beee consoli- dated into one huge amalgamation, thus making the largest concern of its kind now in this country. The leading feature for this season is a realistic production, on a scale of magnificence hitherto unequalled by any show in the world, of a Naval and Military Spectacle, entitled : "With Kitchener to Khartoum,' depicting Great Britain's recent most glorious victorieson the Nile. An unique fearuresof the spectacle is the construction OR novel and ingenious lines of twelve gunbotts, repre- senting the vessels of the Kealifa, and the flotilla of the Sirdar, these being exact lac-similes on a reduced sale of those beats used in the actual conflict. The whole of the vast arena is for the time b-ing converted into a scene representing the neighbourhood of Omdurman,and the banks of the Nile. where the action took place.— Atbara, Omdurman, Khartoum—names already part of the world's history. Cirreet uniforms and arms of nor own brave troops, and dress and weapons of the Khalifa's savage forces, combining with the gunboats bristling wi:h frowning mnules of guns, makes this a most impressive picture. Cowards of 500 persons ars engaged in this striking representation. 460 horses and mules, and a herd of Met:thaws and c Imsls. One of the great features is 100 horses performing in the ring at one time. A series of platforms are erected in the ring, rising tier upon tier, the apes reaching a height of twelve feet, upen which the horses trot in opposite directions at the word of con:mane.</p>
        <p>Special interest wiil be evinc3d on the coming of this lo'g enterprise by the fact that this is the conc.arn that had the high honour ef appearing befsra H.M. the Queon and suite at Balmoral Castle, on Friday. Jun 3 17th of last year. In aidition to Her Majesty there vere also present—Princess Henry of Prussia. Princess Victoria of Wales, Princ Victoria of Schieswig-Holstein. Prince Leopold of Battenberg, Prince Maurice of Battmherg, and Prince Wallemar of Prusaia—as well of the mem- b3rs of the HouFehall, and some 700 of Her Maj3sty's tenantry from the Balmoral, Birkhall, and Ahergeldie estates. There may he truly he said to have been an absolute record attendance of Royalty and Nobility for a Circus Performance—Her Majesty having been deligh- ted with every itam on the long programme, expressing her royal pleasure frequently by applauding, and r•- mairied for thr,e hours and seven minutes from the cornmencement, and at the termulation of tho perfor- mance said: "It is the best performance of its kind I have ever seen." She tuok special ktereEt in the elephant which H.R.H. the Prince of Wales rode on his tour in India, and which was brou,,ht into the vast arena be his Indian att3niant t) make his obesiance to Hr Majesty. A very interestiog item is the fact that Her Majesty, evincing A- strong delight in the aril ics 07. tsvo very dimntive black ponies—a perfectly matche I pair—Lord George Saner immeclia,•eiy ma ie her a pre- sent of them-4 present which she er iciously accepted. There are, as usual, two performances daily, a-id the grand atreet pageant leares the ei-tc.ampment ground at one o'clock. and parades the principal streets—a mag- nificent display of barbaric opulenco and grandeur. Further particulars may be gained from our advertising columns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FUNERAL or. THE LATE MR. WILLIAM HEATON.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL or. THE LATE MR. WILLIAM HEATON.</p>
        <p>On Monday aiternom the remains of the late Mr. William Heaton were interred la the Warrington Ceme- tery with military honnars. The deceas/cl gentleman's long and honourable cmnection with the Volunteers,and the respect in which he wai held WO strikingly avid- encld on this occasion. The esteem which he had gained had also a wider sphere, and thii was alto eloquently demonstrated by the large public attendance at the funeral. Carried to hi; last rating-place by his corm- rades.and surrlunded by numerous sympathetic friends, the end was such as one would wish of all good &amp;limns.</p>
        <p>The Volunteers assembled at th Drill fl di at 1-30. and proceeied t) the late resideece of the deceased half-an- hour later. The funeral cortege was there formed, and proceeded to the Cemetery. Toe bind, under the direc- tion of Bandmaster Armstrong, heact,cl the processioa. Afterwards came toe hearse, at either side of which walked the boarers, Colout-Sergeants Spencer, Wood, Moore, Cliffe, Dunswortle, Millett, and Jolley, and Hospital Sergeant Buckley. The carriages con- taining the &amp;lief mourners were followed by a company numbering fifty with Se.reeant-Majer Sullivan, Sergeant-Instructor B,rker, and several ser- geants, under the command of Lieutenant 13,nnhar 1. Sergeant-Majer Delmar, and seven sergeants from the Barracks were present. A firing party was under the direction of Coleur-Sergeant Wilcock. A large number of people awaited the arrival of the nrocession at tbe Cemetery. At the gates the band play( d the Dead March from "Saul." 'the tuneral eervice, both in the chapel and at the grave-si Jo, was coneuctrel by the Rev 11 V Piget. Toe relatives present were Mr T Heaton (son), and Councillor R Heaton,and Mr John Fleaton (brothers). Among others who attended were Lieutenant-Colonel Ridgway, Alderman Elotehineen.Mr T Lonerlin (borough surveyor),and Messrs Hugh F Greenough, W Beewick, Norman. E Place,G Wallington, J Griffithe,and R Lewis. As it was being borno from the church it was observed that the coffin was cerered with the Union Jack. The deceased gentleman's helmet and, sword had aleo been placed upon it. At the conclusion of the service three voleys were fired over the grave. The afternoon had been cold and fitfully fine, and as the echoes of the last volley dird away the sky became overcast and snow fell gently, covering the gr mind with a white mantle, ard adding a final touch, of impressiveleis to the sad scene. There were a fee, wreaths from the family, one from the staff sergeants and sergeapte of the battalion" As a token of sincere sempathy and regret," and another from Captain Hutchinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>FUNERAL OF THE LATH AI.E. T. DINWIDDIE.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE LATH AI.E. T. DINWIDDIE.</p>
        <p>The funeral i)1 the late Mr. Thomas liinwiddie, tobacconist, Bridge-street, took place at the War- rington Cemet4ry on Saturday • afternoon. There were present Mr F Dinwiddie (son), Mr T Woods (nephew). and Messrs I Bowan, Kenyon, T Sudlow, Shirlaw, Birtles, H F Greenough, C Leigh, T Lone- din, W Howard, Davenport, B Shearer, Fletcher, H E Hawthorn, and others. The Rev J Warnock con- ducted the service. Wreaths were sent by the Mayor (Alderman Wrizht), the widow, daughters, son, Mr Bowen, Warrington Cricket and Bawling Club, Caledonian Association, Mr and Mrs Shirlaw, Mr and Mrs Sudlow, Mr and Mrs McGarty and "Fred." Mr R Johnson carried out the funeral ar- rangements.</p>
      </div>
