      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>PARR HALL DISCOMFORTS.</head>
        <p>PARR HALL DISCOMFORTS.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—Anyone attending the MIISiCSI Society's Concert on Wednesday evening could not fail to notice the dirty and slovenly way the Hall and the approaches are kept. The Hall itself was miserably cold, and it was very no!ic,able that wraps and overcoats were in universal demand in the audience, and the condition of the orchestra must have been very bad.</p>
        <p>Then again, after the conelrt things were not improved by the want of police to regulate the traffic. Carriages standing three and in some cases four abreaet made it difficult to pet away, and I saw in sPveral instances ladies thinly clad scultin,g their car- riages in a cold wind and snow under-foot, suggestive of colds and influenza.</p>
        <p>If such arrangements are possible •at the Musical Society's concerts are they any better at other times, and if so can we wonder At the complaining of the management of the Parr Hall. If the subscribers to the cancerts fal off it will not be for want of appreciation of the chorus and band,for that is excellent and was never b tter I loslieve in the history of the S,ciety, but becaure of the want of coasideration and thoueht for the heal, h and clnafort of the subscri- bers and those who wish to attend. "Yours faithfully. F. MONKS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>INARRINGTON AND DISPENSARY.</head>
        <p>INARRINGTON AND DISPENSARY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the gewrnor3 of the Warring- ton Infirmary and Dispensary V/VI held in (h Council Chamber at the Town Hall on Thursday afternoon. Mr. John IA hite J.P., presided, an there wera present: Revs Canon Willis (Rector), John Yonge, Father Whittle, J Divies, H V Pigot. Drs. Langdale, Rowden, J Guest GYrnall, Councillor Burt A 3, Mears John Fairclough, Gaor.ge DA:n, J Taylor, G Thomps.m. D Fingham, C heirsOn H Leigh, with Mr J L ranstall (hon secretary).</p>
        <p>Letters of ap-ilozv for non-Attendanc. were reid fro:n the president, Mr J 0 Parr, and Mrs PHrrnere.</p>
        <p>71.12 REPORT AND ACCOUNTS</p>
        <p>The CH&amp;IRMAN moved the adoption of the report ani accounts, a gni-ornery of whielo were giv M in our last issue. Briefly stated, the income and expenditure as compared with the year 1897 shows an increase of in- come of £630 94 and a decrease in expenditure of £29 17.-i 81. Mr. White said it fell to his lot to preside on that occasion. They all regrqel the absence of Mr. Parr but it was gratifying to know that he was not kept away throueh illneis but from other cauvs. The geivernors had received a full statement of Vie conlition o the work- ing of the institution during the pest year and full par- ticulars of its fivaneial position. On considering the later he daresay they would think they were ie a fortunate position; at any rate nroch improvel position from last yea,: The expenditure teal been semewhat lees during the yeer. He did not think it was nwieg to any erear economical effort on part of the House Committee but to the feet that there had been feer-r indoor patientL While the expenditure had been. less, the in- come had been more and this was to erene event due to the feet that subscriptions had inereased in a re- markable manner by £B7. This 77;1S wry sets- factory because they always found the sub- scription list a difficult one to deal with in an institution of • that character. Some sub- scriptions fell away and it was not always that other; took their places. The donations had a's* been largely increased. He hoped the public would not think from the satisfactry year's working that they were not in need of all the help they cmild gee The work was cen- stantly increasing, and although they had had a good year they would no doubt have years when the sutler:rip- tions would fall away. He hoped the support whice had hitherto been given would be extended irt the future, especially when they sow the great work it was deine. 07er 5,000 cases were treated last rear, 'and that was eneugh te show that the aid which had been given must be repeated and stutained in the future. If there were any questione respeetine the report and accounts he would be glad to hear them. The any. Cermet WILLIS said be would like to express , his satisfaction at the increase in d )nations amounting 'to nearly £3OO, aed the increase of subscrin- tione" of nearly -2100 which had enabled the Board cf Management to reduce the de- ficit by £4OO. He had been wondering whether it was possible anti advisable to print end peb'ish the accounts before the Hospital Sunday. If that were done it Would enable the clergy to speak with reater effect in pressime the claims of the institution and enable them to speak of the facts as they found theta to be. The report was eublished only a few days after Hospital Sunday, and he would uk to ask whether it was pos- sible to publish it befere ? Mr. TUNSTALL said be was afraid there might be some difficulty. They always had a great diffi.eulty in getting in the trade :men's bills, and it would leave them very little time. Eenrything would of course de- pend upon the date of Hospital Sunday. The Rev. CANON WILLIS said Hospital Sunday was generally fix -ti for the third Sunday in the month. That would leave nearly three months for the preparation of the report.</p>
        <p>Mr. TIINSTALL said he would endeavour to gct the re- peTt ready in time fo,T pitl Sunday. The Rev. CANON WiLus then seconded the adoption of the report and accounts which were unanimously ap- proved of.</p>
      </div>
