        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASE. At the Earle3town Police Station on Tuesday morning before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Henry Fell, of Athol-street, Earlestown. was charged with being drunk and diQorderly and was fined ss. and costs. The charge was Droved by P.C. Gathereole.</p>
        <p>A—MAN KNOCKID- DOWN BY A CAW. On Monday afternoon a man, apparently a stranger was crossing Wargrave-road, near the bridge, when be was knocked down by a horse and spring cart belonging to Mr. E. T: Evans and driven by a lad named Cummings Fortunately the wheel di‘i not past over his body but it was scotcheoll by his chest, and the boy drew the horse up suddenly, the man being abls to crsep away. tie was attended to by several passers by, and after resting he managed to walk away.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT THE VIADUCT WORKS. On Friday morning a lad named John Adams' employed as a screwing boy in the Viaduct Works, met with serious injury whilst following his employment. He was engaged screwing bolts, and had to change his dies but neglected to shift the carriage before doing RO, and in reaching over, one of the long bolts caught the sleeve of his shirt, and drew his arm over, and the revolving of the bolt lacerat, d his arm, over,, and head. It was feared that his arm was broken, but an examination by Dr. Handyside showed this was not so, but his wounds had to be stitched. 1.0.G.T. HOPE OF EARLRSTOwN LODGE. The first meeting of the above was held on Mon- day evening in the New Room, Wargrave-road. After the ordinary business was gone through, ar- rangements were made for a lecture to be given by Bro. Jackson, G.L A., at Es.rlestown, in a week or so. The lodge was then opene Ito the public. Pro- gramme fer the evening: A visit from the Onward Lodge, Ashton. Sister Miss Bowler presided, sup- ported by Bros. Bowler, Blinstone, and Speakman. An excellent programme was gene through of short addresses, recitations, songs, trios, &amp;c. Thanks be- ing tendered to the visitors by Bro. G. Clarke, conded by Sister Williams, an interesting meeting was closed. The ball was crowded. DEATH OF " JOHNNY " DEARDEN. At the age of 37 years, John Dearden, better known as " Johnny " Dearden, has passed away since our last issue. He had been unwell for about a fortnight, from influenza following upon which was inflammation of the lungs, and this brought. about his end. " Johnny " who came of resnectehle parents was an eccentric character, and his chief delight was to pose as a detective, anl an intimate friend of Mr. Caminala of Manchester. Latterly he has not bothered the polies very much, but a few years ago he haunted th-ir footsteps very much,and there is no doubt, in spite of his eccentricity, he wns at times of valuable nspistsnee to the consta- bles. He had peculiar notions in rerr Ito many things, and his familiar figure will no doubt be missed</p>
        <p>DEITFI OF AN OLD NEWTON RESIDENT. Newton has lnst one of its oldest residents by be death of Mr. Fnderick Pennington, keeper of the Town Hall, Newton, which took place on Satur3ay. RC the age of 63 years. The deceased bed been un- well for about five or six woeks, but had been 4t- tended by Dr. Watkins his complaint, however, eoupled with his age left no hone of r,covery. Pennington was very respectably connected and at one time was proprietor of the Newton Nursery Grounds. He was unfortunate, however, and had to give up the place, and for 17 or 18 years he has acted RR keeper of the Newton TOwn Hall. He bls been a resident of Newton for about 40 years, end was well-known by all classes in the township. He leaves a Wide wr. SUDDEN DEATH OF AN INFA.NT The sudden death has been reported to the Earl's- town police of Amos Singleton avc,d 4 months, in- fant son of James Singleton, of 14, Sefton-street, Newton Common. It appears the child began to (cry about midnight on Friday and was very cross off and on until 8 o'clock on Sa-urday morning, when the parents thought it. had gone to sleep. About 10 o'clock the mother went upstairs and found the child lying on its left Rile, apparently dead. Dr. Valentine was sent for. anti be irc. nonuced it to be dead.certifying that death was due to a convulsive fit. The case was model to the Coroner, hut hs did not dee-n en ingneqt, necessary.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL CONSERVITTVE MEETIvG. The annual meeting of the Earlestowe Dolling district of th 4 Newton Division Conservative Asso- ciation was held on Monday nicht in tbe Conserva- tive Club, Earleslnwn. Mr. CB F Borron and was n p Dor t,d by Mr. .1. H. iiottomley, of Vie atinnal Union of Conservative kssoeia.iorpz, Collinciwooi, Mr Barton. Mr W 'Tolland Owan, Mr W A Mawdslay, and Nit. Bailey (local secretary sad treasurar). There was amo lent. stt.ndance. The followinz officers were r'-ele^ted for the ensu- ing year :—Ohairman. Lr CB F Borron ; vice- chairman, Dr Handyside ; eacreAry and traasursr, Mr Bailey; delagat.s to tho Conncil, essrs T den. J Howe, .T Jones, J Smith. T Bowes, and J B Owbridge. The old committee w re-el-c'e I with power to add to their nnmber.—A vote of confid- ence in th. Government was massed en the motion of Mr Collingwoot* seconded by Mr. Barton, and supported at length by Mr Bottomlep</p>
        <p>T. PATRICK'S DA-Y.—MEETING OF IRISIINIWN. A concert was held by the local Irishmen on Saturday, in the League roem,in honour of Ireland's patron saint. There was a large a`tendence, which included 7lfr. P. G. Power, M.P. for East Weter- ford. At the interval this gentleman addressed the gathering en Trish affsire. He referred to the die- eentiona which had unhappily too long deminated Irish milting. and trusted that at the conference to be held in liablin in Easter week everyone would sink his differenees and unite in one body.fie spoke of the general election of 1892, and the nrotnielq made by the Present Geverrim-nt when they came into office. They said that if any erriaVarir!S was brought before them they would be pr-Tared to deal with it, in fact it was sail that Home Reis would be killed by kindness. The first grievance was that an Irish Catholic University should be established. in asking for such a thing they were asking nothing unreasonable. In the advancement of such a wievanee Mr. :Balfour made one of his best speeches. Mr. John Morley name to the conclusion that a Catholic Univereiv was absolutely neleaeary. Tiara was an acknow- ledgment of a grievance by renresentatives of the two parties. Then the Duke of Devonshire did not think that the English peoele need trouble ebout It. for it could wait, as it was not practical pelities. Here they had a Haase ef amyl:ions profeseedly ready and willing, and they immediately threw no the sponge. The second grievance was in regard to the financial relations. The government ap- pointed a Commission consisting of their own men, thorough Britiehere, who brought in e. report that Ireland was taxi two and three-quarter millions more than she ought to be,hrit they did not honour that finding by taken action in the matter. If it had been the other way.they knew what the result would have 'pen...lreland would have been immeli- etely taxed to thefulleat extent lie mentioned;the eunty Conncila Act. and was of opinion that. MI Councils would be only stronger platforms for the agitation for local aelf-governmeut. They had not vet se,•tied the Trish question. They must have Home Rule. (Applause.)</p>
