        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A. ST. TrIMENS POACTIER FINED T PRESCOT,</p>
        <p>At Pr,,sclt Petty SAcmians on 'Tula:lay. hpforp Messra .1. 13.ech.m nod W. Dar', twa 51!.. Ruler's ov‘n named Edward Pmminzton and Thomas Farster. of Bprinefiell- - Thane) Hea,th, wore charr.,ed with win?: n Tint fo• prolurin7 varrw without a Mr J 0 Swift nro- Focuted on behalf of 1,-n.d DPrhy. and °lliad Thomas Rimmar. und.”- qArnroceener. who proved tbn offPnee. which wal committed on the 16:h int. ia a field off Howard's lane, Enchaaren. Dffmdants did not appear, arid were each flood 101 and ennti nr sevAn days.</p>
        <p>DE 4.T11 OF ALDERMAN WBRYDE. - The death of Alderman J Ames Mtflryde, of St• rfftictr,P, which occurred on Wednesday evenine• at his residence, Ba.iker, Cowley Hill, removes one of the men who have heen foremost in establishing and (ley:loping the blrough. The denased alderman was about soventc- four years of age. He came to S. Flplors Ai; a young man, as chemist. with the firm of J C. Gamble and Sons, chemical manufacturers, and later went into partnerchip with Mr. Llewellrn Evans, as Evans and M'Bryde, and on Mr. Evan's retirement the firm took thp name of .Tsnaes Mtßryde and Co , Limited, and tv3.B carricd hy the deceased until the rorml- tion of the United Alkali ComneniPs. M. M'Bryde entered the St. Helens Council in 1874. being eleeted in the room of the late Alderman Bailey. In. 1885 Mr fil'ejt the MaForal ani for seventeen years he was Chairman of the Water Committee. A year laqt Novemb,,, Owing to failing hpAlth, the cleccaed 7,en‘lemln resiened the chatrma.nshin of the Water Committee, and was pte-ted dpeuty-chairman, with Counci!tor F. R. Dixon-Nu tall chairman. Since that time his health has declined, and on MondaY last he was seize 1 with naralysie. and died shQrtly h.fore five o'clock on Wcdnes3ay. The deceased was a Con- servative and a Churchman. He !ewes two daughters and one soy.</p>
