      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CONTEST IN NEWTON WARD.</head>
        <p>THE CONTEST IN NEWTON WARD.</p>
        <p>A meetine in support of the candidature of Dr. Lathim for the Newton Ward was held in the Town Hall Newtoa on Monday evening. The Rev. F. E. Miiler pre- sided and there was a very fair attendance. The Chairman said it was with very great pleasure that he hid accepted the invitation of Dr. Latham's committee to occupy the chair that evening. He did not think that anyoae of them would feel very much diffisulty in deeLliag which of the two candidates claim- ing the suffrages of the electors was the bitter man, ani wnich one should be supported by those who had at heart the interest and good government an I progress of the district. lie had been endeavouring to ascertain what reasons were put forward for supporting the candidate on the other side. As far as he could ascertain those qualifications were represented be a oipher. (Laughter.) He could not say that any good reason: had been put forward that Mr. Simpson was the man to return to the Council erceot teat he had been on the Council already. One reason why he should net be supported was that on .th expiration of his term of office to which he was last elected, a requisition was sent to him asking him, in accordance with the usual custom, to hold a public meeting, and at that meeting to give some account of the trust which was placed in his hands eome three years ago. That was a proper request, and Mr. Simpson very courteously replied saying that he did not intend to hold a meeting, and anyone who wanted a re- pert of his actions must get their information from the newspapers. He (the speaker) did not think anyone would care te buy up the paptirs for the past three years, and wade through them to find out what his ac- tient hid been, and when a requisition of tint kind had been refused, the electors had a right to look for some- eody who retornised that they ought to have some ceu- sideration. Wnile there was no reason why MeSimpson should be supported, oa the other hand they must all feel there were sufficient reasons why DeLethem should receive the hearty support oi the electors of that ward. They wanted new blood on the Council and a new spirit infusing into the conduct of thins. Another reaton for supporting Dr. Ltteam was that he cams f trw tot as a Labour candidate, and he was the choice of the District Trades Council, and when the electors sent him to the District Council he would make it his duty in particular to lock after the interests of labour. It would be very difficult to find oat from the papers what Mr. Situps en hid beeredoinz: Their Council was a secret Council, they had practically a double set of meetings. They hae adopted the device of putting ail the members of the Council on the com- mittee, and the business was conducted at these committee meeting's to which reporters were not admitted. Then there was the Council meet- ing to which reperters were admitted and they want solemnly through the minutes and confirmed them, and unless a question was askied,nr some point of order Waked the public Council meeting lasted any time from 5 ink- rites to a quarter-of-an-hoar. Thus all the business of the Council was done in private, and it might be, in secret, and all the public Council had to do was to confirm what tvid been done at these secret meetings. If Dr. Latham was sent to the Council as a Labour candidate it would he his duty to try and break up these arrangements. They wanted a new spirit and new blood upon the Cluncll, and if they could get a new element on the Coulee. thsy could not And a gentlem La more fitted to int-oil:ice that element than Dr. Latham. (Hear, hear.) He was a gentleman of great abllity of fearless and undaunted courage, a man of independent cenvic- tions, a man who would go to the Council net to be a mere nenentity, not to attach himself to any clique to vote as the teadsrt tell him to do, bet in all that con- cerned the interests; of the working man of the district. to werk independently for the district at large. (Ap- pianee ) Dr. Latham said the chtirosen had covered very use- ful ground. He had told them that the Ctuncil c on- ducted its affairs in stcret, a hole and corner mewing was tht best expressioe they could Apply to the meetings et the Urban D;strict Council of Newton-in-Mtkarfisld. One ef the tin tstiens he should bring before the Council, if elected, would b 3 this question of meeting in comintttee. fie contendee ttat inseeing in secret was merely a farce. When the deputation waited upon him to ask him t come forward as a candi late, they p.esented elm a pregramme of meatures which they believed to be abs entely nettessary for the geed government of the township. On looking over that pro- gramme he hai no hesitation in ;weep leg it in toto, aid he stood to rise or fall by thaw menilestro. He was in favour of evening meetines of the C eincil. On the ceiestion of street making the Council, he said got a frightful snubbing from the Lecal Governevnt Board After that they applied ti the firm of Richard Evans and ere to come and pave Clarence-street. He presumed Richard Evans ane Co saw there was an opportunity of savinz a little m my, and pave I the street. But in his opinion, it was not proeterly paved, and would be a isnurce of trouble to the Council in a very short. time. Th at was not the only street at fauit. In the Newton I ward they had streets in which it was done in a very !unsafe manner. and he blamed the Council for permit- ! tine such a stales of things. On the qae=tien or afire I library he said the Viaduct Institute library wW a private one and no matter how much patching 'they did with a private institution, they ! could not make it have % permanently satisfactory I character. He wee decidedly of opinion teat the town- ship sheald nrevide a free library of its own, and not only so but thee should have, in addition, a good Tech- nical school. He contended that Technical educaelon in teis teweseio was a very unsatisfectore element. The building was nu-it appropriate and the applienees and general equipment of the institution were not favourable to the development of Technical education. He must confess when he looked at the Technical Instruction Com- mittee and etudiei its composition, he heaved a good sigh for it. He was also in favour of pnhlic swimming teethe. With regard to the water supply, they would notice le his address that he looked with °leisure and satisfaction on the fact that the Council I had at last decided to do something in that direction. For over 30 years negotiations Ihad been conducted with the Railway Company , respecting, the water supply, and they were in the same l position new, as they were at the commencement. The days of privtte water supeey were dying out, and he was clad that the Council was at last doing something towards bringing about this supply of water. What concerned them was what hat the Council done? They had bored to n deteh of 460 fe,t, but they had not been able to get 200,000 gallans a day out of it. On the question of gas workers' wages, he said this work was of a very laborious character, and in his opinioe very unhealthy. On October 3rd lest year, the Gas and Water CornTnitese had before them a tabulated state- ment which was prepared by a sub-commit- tee of wages paid at Wigan, Warrington, Leigh, Runcorn, Atherton and Tyldesley, and the reeomeemdation this cub-committee made was that the wages be increased from 4s. 30 per turn of 12 hours ti 41. 61. In addition they got a penny per 1,000 feet of ens carbonised. He did not know where the intellieence of the sub-Cornriettee came in, fer he never heard of gee being carbonised. The O'cleam workers spoke of coal carbonised and they were correct. (Laughter) He drew a comparison between the gas workers of Newton and the fz,.s workers of Oldham, and said that if the Oldham men worked the same hours as the Newton men they would get £2 12s. 61. at the same rate. Speaking in regard to the work of the Highways and Sanitary Committee, he said they had it upon the aathnrity of the Medical Officer of Health that some of the streets of the township were in an infamous condi- tion. Mr. Collingwood said it was not their fault, it was the fault of the gentleman who came down from London and gave them that snubbing; ha would not give them the meney. On the question of tee high infantile mortality, he said Ur. Collingwoxi dii not know what he was talkine about when he made an accusation atrainet the public, in seven that the infantile mortality was their own fault. It was smote- lous because it implied that the parents of the township did not take care of their children. He (the speaker) hoped the electors would look after the matter in their own interests, and see that their streets were not in an infamous condition, as nothing contributed to the suffer- ing of children at having to waddle about in the mud. ' The new school was built on a night soil depot, at leer; it wee built noon ea midgets heap, and he believed it went by the name of " Midlen School." He urged the adoption of the waste water system fer their closets, as it would abolish tee pails,, the carts and the mixing grounds. The State had cast upon them the responsibility of giving them the 'sewer nf recording a vote. It expected them, as elec- tors of Newton Ward to record their vote faithfully, fearlessly according to the dictates of their own con- sc'ence. They had before them two candidates ant they melt go to the ballot box and give their vote according to the dictates of their couscience. (Applause.) The following question was handed un :—`, Will the Library boat Etriestewn or at Newtrn " ? Dr. Lath tm said he knew there we, a great feud between Newton and Etrlestown, arse he was sorry there was this bend. Whatever was built in the township, should as far as possible be in such a poelleoe as to be used by the greatest number of-people. The position of a free library was a very important one. They wanted to get that library al near to the centre of the population us they petsibly could. He had come to the conclusion that the most fitting place for a good library and a Technical school would be close to Cross-lane. If anybtdc Ind any other place to mention he was ready to take it into coß4de-ati,m; (Rear, hear.) Mr. W. Speakman said the electien had caused a flitter in the dm:et:otos of Newton. There was too much class monopoly on the District Council. Tney intended to Sce ire the victery in that ward and they nelieyed it was already won. That was the first time teere had bern a fieht on straight deer lines ; it was labour against the classes. He moved "That this patillc meet- in tof electors of the Newton Ward having heard the views of Dr. Latham en local affiirs, have pleasure in adopting him as a fit and preper person to represent Newton Ward on the Urban Dastrict Council."</p>
        <p>M. Lydiard seconded and the untion was carried almos'a unanimously. - _</p>
        <p>The minting afterwards dispersed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The first round has now no terrors for the Wirrin:lton players, who have passed safely throigh the ordeal with Barrow. They found their opponents ia a determined mood, and had rather a warm time of it to wards the close, but nevertheless the first-league club managed to obtain a majority of 10 points--which may be consi- dered a sitisfactory margin. They wera scarcely ten points the batter team, but that does not matter in lb e least. They will probably find their proper level to-day at Oldham.</p>
        <p>Notwithatanding the fact that Old Sal shone briefly o'pr the sc3ne, and tie day was an almirable one for the pursuit of f ootball, the " gate " was most disappointing. Double pries to watch Warrington play was a bit too much for the enthusiaqm of the supporters, and consequently there were no 3.000 present, which is a nevelty for a cup :;ie at Warrington. However it is but the natural sequel to the ignoble doings of the team during the sason. Some slight consolation is to be had from the fact that " gates " ruled low at all Northern Union matches, many of which were of so farcical a kind that the public was content to sty at home and wait for the evening pap 4..</p>
        <p>Cup-tiers are genera:ly supposed to be inferior to ordinary league games as expositions of Rugby foce.ball, usually because excitement plays havoc with combination. At Warrington, however, there wai no excitement to do the havoc-playing, and what WAS worse there was no combination. I did think Warrington would be able to show something like an understanding with (me another against Barrow, hut no! they just rompd about in the old sweet way. The Barronians were slightly more erratic and vari- able or it might bethat Werrington's holiday trip to Old- ham would have hid to be postponed. Even Bate could not drill the men behind him into anything like systema- tic play, and the game was tha3 of a very poor order— even for a cup-tie. There is little to be sail ahout it,bat duty demands that something must apoear in this colu nn. A fine large breeze, one of those mirrow-petrifying blasts from the North East, had a lot to say in the coMest, and at the same time did not render the spnotAtors par:lcull.7. joyful. Bite's mill good luck stuck te him and he had the pleasure of set- ing the wind behind him for the first part of the game, and good use was 'nide of the circueistarne by the homesters. Mason kicked a g oal early on and then Fair- hurst surprised everybody, himself included, by dropping 3 second goal with the greatest apparent ease. These were welcome points but the home speca- tors hoped for something better. Bate pined rather badly once or twice and chenees went a-begging. A really god piece of combined play by the home backs ended in flockenhull racing over at the corn T, bat the touch-flax went up and the try was disallowed. War- rington continually pressed and at last the Barrow de- fence was pierced, Bate dodging over the line fro:n a scrimmage. Just before the interval the vi,itori were penalised and Mason raised Warring 'roa'rs in ijoricy to nine points with another fine goal.</p>
        <p>It took Barrow some time to obtain any advantage on changing ecds as the Warrington men played with con- aderable dash. Suoeriority forward eventually g'ol3 Barrow the upper band and they beg tn to cause trnuble. At the moment when they were showing up well they had the misfortune to lose the servicet' of Hartley at half-back, and thus weakened in the p ack their chances were leesened considerably. Bet wita only s 4ven far wards. and Treweake in the centre, Barrow had the best of matters and were unfortunate in not getting points. Several drops for goal went very near, and Harnblett succeeded in getting over from an opening made by Bowden, but again the toach flag was in the way. Fall time had netrly arrived when Hamblett took another drop at goal, and the ball Ktriking the cr ass-bar managed to fall on the 6e/it side, Barrow thus escaping the indignity of being niltei.</p>
        <p>In many respects the game did nnt resemble a cup.tie. Thera was little excitement manifested, aid the players almost all went about their work AS if tie encounter was merely a "-Merrily!' I most ruito the lore on the good show ttley made, e;pecially forward. In this department they were immensely superior to the Warrington .eight, w'4o Seem to 114,1,7(1 lost all their strength and dash. In s silly of the scrimmig‘l they were pushed right off the ball, and if they got it at times they were slow in heeling out. Had Barrow been at full strength in the second half it is safe to say that they would have come nearer to WArra.c:totV4 se.ore thin they did. Both set; of backs w ,sre pa -sr in combination tlrytgh each mart did go3ll work iniividuOv. Hallam piayed an excellent game for the homesters as did Har- ris and Ashuret. Bate was off-ocher in some re3pec•.s. notably in passing. OF the forw srcls Elea. Mason, and Nsylor were most prominent. Bawker, Bowden, and Wade all rendered excellent service in the Barrow rear division. while Trewerikg was a shining light among the forwards.</p>
        <p>There has men a slight alteration in the Warrington team selected oppose Oldhim. Duck- wurth, Mason, and Eden fall out a-id are replaced lo? Wilcox, Cunningham and Townend. I think Wilcox at half, and Ashur at centre will be an ifnprovom.nt, but against mull; speady and clever plAyers as the O'cl- him back; I should have preferred Duckworth to 13,)ck,Inhull. He has had the most experience and know.; hoc; to mark his man batter than young flookinhull, theugh lam not going to disparage the latter. He playei smartly at times on Saturday,</p>
        <p>The heal junior teams do not set9ll to bavo cere themselves with glory in the cup-ties. Class trAd and they were defeatpd with ease by their more plwerhil opponents. At Swint m, Fletcher Russell's were quite outclasfo 1, but thea they must not think to put thert- oa the same level as one of the nest team in Lancashire. St. Mary's made the best fight oF the lot and were not very badly blaten by Bowling. The wooden-spoonists crake enjoyed themselwas at the ex- pense of Latchford Rangers-- shout whom, by the way. I suppose we shall hear nothing more.</p>
        <p>There will be slml bes,tar gams la the soconl round, and among the tit-bits may be mentioned Leigh v. Runcorn, Broughtou Rangers V. Hull Kingston Ravers, Swinton v. Hunslet and Oldham v. Warrinzton. Any of these games will be worth seeing, and I haps the latter won't be quite the runaway far Oldham it expected to be.</p>
