        <p>THE COMMITTEE AND TUE WORKS COMPErunoN.- ,</p>
        <p>Thom has be9a a lot ot talk in the town ahant ths de- cision of th 3 Coinmit•:e.3 to discmtinus the Works Com- petition, and most of tha people I hive milt— in fact all with the excaption of club okli cials—are against the decision. And for my own part I think it is a great mistake to let the t.; inpetitioc drop. The alleged reason is that the field would be ser- iously damage i by the game: neco.;sitated. I have my own opinion about the reisoa for this extraordinary step, but I will not express it. I will say this, howevei., that the rsason advanced is is totally inadequate 0.19. Thp gronticl would be none the worse after a tow weeks rest. Can anyone prove that the old second ground abutting on the Causeway wai one bit th 3. wori,e for competition na,t3hes played on it last season ? My a good portion of at is superior to many foNliall fields yet, notwithstanding the trampling it his had this season, Other clubs play warks c)inestition matches on their footeall grounds without doing the turf any hurt, and why should not we? S. much for the argument about the grouad.</p>
        <p>I suppose there are nr) two ways of lookinz at the fact that the plub is badly inwanof a few raur players. Eff)rta at imp min them have s 9 far not been atten- deg with dazziina succosa, and are the committee going to deliberately throw away their lant chance of unearth- ing latent talent in the town? All the promising lads are not in the junior teams. There are many who have it in them who might never take to football were it not</p>
        <p>for the stimulus of a Werks Competition. The same argument aoplies with ()plat force to spectators. There are many people who nave never sown any interest in football, who become interested in the doings of their work-mates and eventually take the football fevor. Thetuestain the Work 4 Competitions have so far been pro- fitable from a financial point of view, as well as benefi- cial to those wort iy institutions, the Infirmary aud the Narsing As4ecia:inn. Can the club afford to throwaway a single source of revenue? I don't think so, juiging by the proceeds of this seasen's matchls. Were the Cab nittee to canvass their members only—leaving out the thousands of supporters paying weekly at the turn- stile—l am convinced that they vvoul find an over- whelming majo:hy in favour of the continuance of such a popular thin; as the Works Competition. And the majority am me the rank and file of football enthusiasts in the town would be even greater. There is yet time for a reaoneideration of the matter, and I hopethe C mn- mittee will take the opportunity and no: be guilty of soch a piece of culpable foly as the abandoament of the Works Competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PIONEER LECTURES AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>PIONEER LECTURES AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening the third of the course of lec- tures promoted by the Newton-in-Makerfild LeCtUro3 Committee. on "The gr owtta of the British Empire," was given in the Town 11111, Etrleetown, by the Rev. T. J. Lawrence, M.A., L.L.D. his subject being "The struggle with France for &amp;lonia! Empire." Mr. Robert Stone presilel, and said if they would permit him he would take their memmies back to a month ago, when Mr. Yule Oldham delivered his lecture on " The field ot Empire." They would remember Mr. Oldham showed them, aided by his excellent maps,how the Pncenecians, a maritime rice wile belonged to the eastern pert of the Mediterraeeurn sea, first began P.O make long voyages and discovered new countries. Time fever for discovery Mr. Oldham explained spread westwards; first it caught on with the Greeks and then the Romans, and the later still, in the 13th century Marco Polo as born in Venice, and he commenced to explore eastwards, hat still tee fever of discovery continued in a westerly direction, and century later it caught on with the Portugese and then the Spaniards, and th n, he thought:, when Mr. Oldham Came t) that part of eis lecture they all experieneed a feeling of regret that the great Continents and the greater p trt of the world had Won discovered before England got her chance. France had gone on pretty much the same linee, and our interests had at times Meshed with theirs. Within Tone years matters in Burrnah, on the West Coast of Africa and Fashoda have had to be adjusted. Thera were othe,. questions between the two countries yet remaining to be settled, but as we have been at peace with Frenee the last 80 years, he thought we might reasonably consider that these other matters would be adjusted satisfactorily also. (App'ause.) Be hoped and thought there was every chance that France might long continue to be our most charming neighbour in spite of the little jealousy which might exist with regard to our Colonial posses- sions. (Applause.) The Lecturer said as far as he ceuld gather from the remarks of the Chairman and from a study of the syllabus of his friend Mr. Oldham, he should have to I;azin pretty much where he loft off, and should have to s'erdy the French and English Celonies, not the whole world over, but in North America and to show how the two nations formed their characteristics and to lead up to the final expulsion of the French from the North American Continent. That was a wonderful story, and a story which could not be pressed into the limits of an hour's lecture. They had been told with perfect ac- curacy that we mere late in tile path of disc overy, but though we were late be thou:flat our gleanings were by no means t be despised. But 13,fore he began to talk about the relative amount of discovery done by the races of modern Ea*ope, he ()eget to give a meea of praise to those heroic No7sernen from the shores of Norway. Columbus did not discover America, it was discovered by one Ericsson, the son of Eric the Red, one of the earl;eot Christian converts amongst toe Nerweg4en people. Ericsson sailed from Iceland with a crew of 30 men towards the West, and coasted down until he got (.1):vn to whit is now called Novia Sentia.and he found in the early autumn the woodlands filled with the vine,and an Italian amongst hit crew named it " Vittelaed the G,c,d." Great glory justly settled round the name and career of Columbus, but he had no information of the existence of the c.,,reat American ceuntries. Jahn Gabot in the yiar 1497 sailed intethe Atlantic over its Northern portion and touched doubtless upon Labrador. Be came back to E egland with the story of what he had disceverni. The net year he went aeain with his son sebasian Ganot. The voyages of 1477 and 1478 were the voyages in which Euroirals, c)neps.ratively in laird Tra times, set foot upon the laud of North America. 11+stoen proceeded by aid ne maps and limelight views to trace step by step the colonisieg of portions of I America by the Euelish and French, describing how the various tracts CAM, into the respective posse ssian of the two nations. He gradually led sap to the acquisition of Canada by the French, and c included by an excellent and interesting dascPiplon of the capture of Quebec bv General Wolfe, and the retirement of the French from Canadian t-Tritery.—Tne usual votes of thanks were accorded at the close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WM? RINGTON.</head>
        <p>WM? RINGTON.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON "FOODS."</p>
        <p>On Monday evening another of the series of lec- tures on agriculture vvas given in the Church Insti- tute, Daresbury, by Ar. Edwards, on behalf of the Cheshire County Council. There was a fair attend- ance. The chair was taken by Mr. T. S. Burgess, of Higher Walton. Tne subject was "Riols, especially those adapted for milk production." At toe con- clusion an interesting diicussion took place, in which many joined.</p>
        <p>CELINA TEA SERVICHIS, Dinner Services, Toilet and Trinket Sets. Cheapest honse.---J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, Bridge.street, Warrington. WARRINGToN MARKETS. •</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Cara Market on Wedne.day new wheat (white) fetched 33 7d to 33 9d (70 lb. to the bushel); red wheat, 33 61 to 33 8d •' new oats, 23 2d to 2441. Flour best seconds. per 2401 b., 183 61 ; Bran, 11Ad ta is lid per 201 b. Prices at the Provision Mark.3t were as follows :—Fresh butter, is al and la 41 oar lb; fresh eggs,l4 and 15 for is; fowls, 43 6,1 to 5s per couple; ducks, 63 Od ; Eng- lish beef, 51 to 101; mutton, 6d to 9J; veal, 7d to lid; pork, 6d to 81.</p>
        <p>- CYCLES.—Apecial Ladies and Gents Machines at £7 15s Sea Windows, J. S. GARLA.ND, Bridge-st., Warrington. MARRIA.GE OF MR. W. W. WHITTLE.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, at the Paris Church, tne wedding took place of Mr. W. W. Whittle, and Harriet, eldest daughter of Mr. William Bramh all, of Man- chester-road. The parties are well known in the town, especially the bridegroom,owing to his readi- ness to give his services as a humorist. The officia- ting clergyman was the Rector, the Rev. Canon Willis. Ole bride, who was charmingly attired in a heliotrope silk dress, with toque to match, was given away. by Mr. F. J. Whittle, (brother of the brilegroom). She was attended by .Miss Bramtiall (sister of the bride) and Miss E. Whittle (sister of the bridegroom) as bridesmaids, whilst the Misses Dorothy and Vera Collin (nieces of the bridegroom) acted as trainbearers. All carried shower bouquets the gift of the bridegroom. Mr. Thomas Bramhall (brother of the bride) acted as bast man. A recep- t'ton was held at the home of the bride,a large num- ihr sitting down to the wedding break f a9t. Many costly Nvdditig present.] have been received.</p>
        <p>UNIM, TELE OI,OOKroa GOOD BOOTS.—Ad. BRICK .)TREET lissioi SAL&amp; OF WORK. _</p>
        <p>On Friday and eitveirday last a vary sueces- sf at sale of work was held in the Mission Hill, Brick- street. Mr. Thomas Barton opened the Sale of Work on the Friday and in a few well chosen re- 'marks, expressed his sympatny with the object of the eff mt. The large room always bright and pleas- -ant, presented an exceptionally bright aud pictur- (Nig As appearance. The prepiritione had been undehaken by sub-committees, who vied with each other in their endeavours to make a good display. Tee refreshment stall presented an inviting appear- ance and was well patronised by trite visitors. A department presided over by the married ladies had a largo di:play of domestic and useful arGicles while the der,artment, superin.tendel by the young ladies had an abundance of ornamental and fancy articles to show. Au advertisement stall presided over by the young men had the usual display of do- mestic necessities, and did a brisk busi- ness and a stall of useful articles repre- sentel the efforts made by the Suaday school scholars. On b3th evenings a humorous minstrel sketch entitled" Mischievous Mosea" was spirited'y rendered, and their efforts t) amuse were rnucti -appreciated. Other sources of attractions were two washing competitioas, open to men only, which ware held in the spacious b isement under the hall. Tne Young Men's Committee, who had these in hand, were indebtel to Messrs. Lever Bros. of Port See eght, for the valuable prizes which they kindly presented for these competitions, which consisted of a case e!ectro plated spoons and sugar tongs, and a large b)x of assorted soaps of their own manufacture, which were duly awarcled to the 611 C• cessful competitors. Much merriment was created by the eaer4etlc and amusing tiforts of tie com- petitors. The nett amount realized fro-n all sources of this effort was £6l, which reult was most grwifying to the workers.</p>
        <p>The GLEN PUY AND STRA.THMILL ettuated in the finest Whtiky pradming district of S(.b.otland are the property of W. and A.. GILBIA. These Whiskies are kept absoiutely unblended in Her Alajesty's Bonded Warehouses to. Mature, and are sold by W. and •A. GILBUY'S AQlgari In every town at 3s. 6d. per bottle.— Advt.</p>
