        <p>LECTURE om Nitrzrc.—On Mlnday evening, at the weekly meeting of the Wesley Guild, held in the school- room, MiSS Mary Bennett gavo an intere-tine: lecture on " Mendtlssohn" and Mid 3 L,ly Mason one on "Hanoi-4." The chair wai taken by llts3 .fennie Bennett (Lrad- hurst), who gave an amusing addre3s as to the effects of music on animals. Miss Ada Birtles c intributed pianoforte selEctions from Mendelssohn's " Songs with- out Words," illustrative of a portion of Miss Bennet's lecture, and Miss BennAtt sang a soprano solo trona the oratorio" St. Paul." Miss Mvion favoured with an illus- tration of Handel's music, with pianoforte and 'cello ac- companiments. The two lady lecturers at the close of the meeting sang the duet," 0 lovely nein° " (" Judas Maccabous.") On the proposition of Councillor lr:tiinett, a hearty vota of thanks wis accorded to the lecturers, and to Miss J. Bennett for her presence in the chair.</p>
        <p>P.S. A.,AT TH Y. M.C. A A meeting of the above society was held in the lecture ball, on Sunday, Mr. e. England presiding. The Rev. C. Finch gave an excellent address entit- led" Work and worksri." The following songs were effectively rendered by Mrs. Atherton :—" The Dream of Paradise," and "The Land of Little Chil- dren." Mr. Taker and gr. Cloug,h also took part in the •meeting. Mr. F. Hickman was the accompanist. and, Mr. Atherton presided at the organ, —A social evening was held on Monaay at the Y.NI C.A.. There was a large attendance, and the President (Mr. William Hickman) presided, supported by Mr. W. Roberts and Mr. George Holford. The errangementS for the distribution of the refreshments were in the hands of the secretary (Mr.W.Clegge). The follow- ing programme was gone through, frequeut encores proving that the items were keenly appreciatel : Duet, Mr. F. and Miss Hickman; songs, " The Swallows." "Going to Kildare,' "Barney O'Hea," by Miss Christian; Mr. Wilkinson, zither harp solo. Miss P. Hamer sang "The Lost Chord," and "Darby ; and Joan." Mr. R. Wood sang "Good Company." Mr. Grimshaw " Mary of Argyll," and Mr. W IVo )(la H' They all love Jack," and " Sailing " kir II M Abh- ton was asked for anathEr reading, his first item being entitled " Chanze of Trea•ment,. " The instru- mental items were—Pianoforte solo by Mr. G. B. Holford and two violin solos, "Broken Melody," and "Tarantella," (Papini), by Mr. Elgar Clare. ' The accompanist was Mr. Frank Hickman.</p>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL. At the Cairo-streat chapel on Sunday night, th 3 RIV. W. H. Drummond preached a sermon on "Toe social teaching of Charles Kingsley," taking for his text the words, ' The Lord bath giv:M me the tongue of the teamed that I should know how to sneak a word to him that is weary." In the cows, of hi 3 remarka. the rear. gentleman said Charles Kingsley was a man of the very best culture, and a great lover of outdoor sports. He was not merely 'a friend of the religious, but had the Rood word ef those without the Church. They want t 3 hear him preach, and returned to their work with manly thoughts. He spoke iu the plabaest way to the workers of England, and practised what he preached. At the time cholera broke out, he gither:A about him bands of voluntary workers, and did much geed and valuable) work.</p>
