        <p>Indeed, the purity and the exceptional nutrient properties of Bovril have been .vouched for by the greatest analysts and the best known authorities on dietetics and medicine. What, then, must be avoided I The dubious" table delicacies" ( so-called) which have not passed the strict tests of scientific analysis; prepared meats oy doubtful origin; and the self-styled concentrated meat-foods made in this country, where the price of prime ox beef renders it too expensive an ingredient for the purpose— a fact which suggests a temptation to substitute inferior materials. The beef of Australia, 1 New Zealand, and South America (where oxen are plentiful and pasture is abundant) is of the finest quality. " Bovril is prepared from these oxen, and the supply • is so con- siderable as to enable the manufacturers to use the best materials at a practically nominal cost. - Ftij 17 . AND PURITY uia</p>
