        <p>PRUDENTIAL ASSURANCE COMPANY, LIMITED. CHIEF OFFICE: HOLBORN BARS. LONDON. SUMMARY OF THE REPORT PRESENTED AT THE FIFTIETH ANNUAL MEETING, HELD ON 2len MARCH, 1899. ORDINARY BRANCIL—The number of Policies issued during the year was 64.708 essuring the sum of £6.420,580 and producing a New Anne al Premium Income of £353,113 The Premiums received daring the year were £2,967,501 being an increase of £193,237 over the year 1897. The Claims of the year amounted to £975,012 ' The number of deaths was 5,191 and 2,535 Endowment Assurances matured. The number of Policies in force at the end of the year was 534,138. INDUSTRIAL BR A.NCIT.—The Premiums received during the year were £4.950,756 being an increase of £!67,165. The Claims of the year amounted to £1.891.039, The num- , tier of Deaths was 198,308 and 2,1.61 Endowment Assurances matured. The numberof Free Policies granted during the year to those Policy-holders of five years' standing, who desire to die- continue their payments, was 66.379, the number in force being 604,564. The number of Free Policies which became , Claims during the year was 12,231. The total number of Policies in force at the end of the year was 12,919,679: their average duration exceeds eight and a , half years. 9he Assets of the Company, In both branches as shewn in the Balance Sheet, are £33,599,708, being au increase of £3.161,371 over those of 1897. Public attention having been for some time past directed to questions of' thrift and provision for old age, the Directors have had under coesideration how they can further assist those Policy holders who from age and diminished earnings find some difficulty in maintaining the payment of Premiums on their Policies, and they have therefore made provision for all Policyholders in the Industrial Branch who have been. assured for twenty five years to be free from the payment of further Premiums as they attain the age of 75, This altera- tion takes effect at once, and has of course necessitated a considerable-addition to the Reserves. The Shareholders will be glad to learn that the Staff Provi- dent Fund, which was established last year on the occasion of the celebration of the Company's Jubilee, has been heartily appreciated by the outdoor St:ft for whose benefit it was founded, and a large proportion haee become contributors. The total amount standing to the credit of the Fund at the end of the year was £58,212 183„ and the number of the con- tributors was 7,160. GENERAL BALANCE SHEBT OF THE PRUDENTIAL ASSURANCE COMPANY. LIMITED, ON THE 31st DECEMBER. 1893. LIABILITIES. £ e. d. Shareholders' capital 1.000,000 0 0 , Ordinary Branch funds Industrial Branch fund 14,538 952 13 5 Reserve fund Claims under life policies admitted £33,599,708 6 0 ASSETS. £ e. d. British Government Securities (23,155,00) Consols) Indian and Colonial Government Securi- ties Railwa/ and other Debentures and Deben- ture Stocks 2,176.493 18 1 Loans on County Council, Municipal and other rates Freehold ground rents and Scotch feu duties Freehold and leasehold property 1,934,411 16 10 Mortgages Railway, gas, and water stocks Suez Canal Shares Telegraph and othec shares... . Metropolitan Consolidated stock, and City of London b nds Bank of England Stock 209,559 18 6 Foreign Government securities Reversions and Llte Interests.. Loses on the Company's Policies Rent charges 148,164 0 1 Outstanding premiums 3E0,525 9 10 Cash in hands of Superintendents and Agents' balances Outstanding interest and tents 241,243 17 0 Cash—On deposit. on entreat accounts, and iu hand £33,599,703 6 0 ----- BDGAIt HORNE, Chairman. HENRY HARDEN t. Directors. W. T. PUGH, THOMAS C. DEWEY, Ie:ILLIAM HUGHES. } Joint General Managers. FRED. FISHER, W. J. LANCASTER, Secretary. We have examined the Cash transactions, Receipts and Payments, effecting the accounts of the Assets and Invest- ments for the year ended December 31st, 1898, and we find the same in good order and properly vouched. We have also examined the Deeds end Securities. Certificates. Nt , repre- senting the Lesets and Investmente set out in the above account, and we certiiy that they were in possession and safe custody as on December 31st, 1898. DELOITTE. DEVER, GRIFFITHS 8c Co. 14th February, 1829. District Offices: 69, Sankey. street, Warrington. Superintendent, J. T. HOWLAND.</p>
